# 142.
t(142,'1968-02-12','I. M. Gelfand','P000228-P000230','Positive functionals on countably normed algebras',r'On P000229 Gelfand asks which countably normed algebras admit a character-space integral representation for positive functionals. The comparison is with the commutative Banach-algebra representation theorem and Bochner\'s theorem. Completeness, continuity, commutativity and the class of measures are suppressed in the notebook; those distinctions control the modern moment problem.')
i(142,'P000229','definition','Countably normed algebra and continuity of multiplication',r'The notes write \(\|xy\|_i\leq\|x\|_k\|y\|_\ell\) for a countably normed ring \(R\).',r'A countably normed complex algebra is an algebra with a Hausdorff locally convex topology defined by a countable family of norms. The displayed condition, interpreted as: for each \(i\) there exist \(k,\ell\) such that it holds for all \(x,y\), implies joint continuity of multiplication. Completeness makes this a Fr\'echet algebra. For the subsequent moment question one additionally needs a commutative algebra, a continuous involution and a specified continuous dual.',r'The quantifiers and the distinction between complete and incomplete algebras are supplied. No assumption that every norm is submultiplicative is inserted.',fate=r'The definition is the setting of the question, not a new theorem. Modern comparisons distinguish the topology from the positivity cone; see \citet[\S\S2.2--2.3]{infusino2024moment}.',status=r'this remains standard terminology, with completeness and involution hypotheses to be stated separately.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',refs=['infusino2024moment'])
i(142,'P000229','theorem','Positive functionals as integrals over Hermitian characters',r'A theorem labelled Gelfand--Naimark says \(f(x^*x)\geq0\Rightarrow f(x)=\int x(M)\,d\sigma(M)\), with \(M\) symmetric maximal ideals.',r'For a unital commutative complex \(C^*\)-algebra \(A\), every positive linear functional \(L\) is uniquely \(L(a)=\int_{X(A)}\chi(a)\,d\mu(\chi)\), where \(X(A)\) is the compact space of unital characters and \(\mu\) is a finite positive regular Borel measure. Every character is Hermitian: \(\chi(a^*)=\overline{\chi(a)}\).',r'The precise \(C^*\)-algebra version is used to avoid attributing the assertion to arbitrary normed noncommutative rings. The notebook\'s symmetric maximal ideals correspond to kernels of Hermitian characters in the commutative setting.',fate=r'This is the classical representation paradigm explicitly invoked by the notes. Its infinite-dimensional moment analogues require additional positivity or continuity assumptions; \citet[Abstract]{schmudgen2018moment}.',status=r'the stated classical version is established; the literal phrase ``normed ring'' does not specify adequate hypotheses.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',refs=['schmudgen2018moment'],confidence='medium')
i(142,'P000229','question','Axioms ensuring moment representations in countably normed algebras',r'\ru{Перенос --- ? (Не всегда: надо охар. аксиомат. класс).} The question asks when the preceding representation extends.',r'Fix a commutative unital Fr\'echet \(*\)-algebra \(A\), and let \(X_h(A)\) be its continuous Hermitian characters with the weak topology. Which additional hypotheses on \(A\), its topology and a continuous functional \(L\), positive on \(a^*a\), ensure a finite positive Radon measure \(\mu\) on \(X_h(A)\) with \(L(a)=\int\chi(a)\,d\mu\) for every \(a\)? Cylinder measures and Radon measures must be distinguished.',r'This fixes one natural interpretation of the incomplete question. It is not claimed to be the unique intended class of countably normed rings.',cls='C',fate=r'\citet[Abstract]{schmudgen2018moment}, circulated in December 2017, treats cylinder-measure representations under stronger positivity or Carleman assumptions. \citet[\S\S2.2--2.3]{infusino2024moment}, circulated in January 2023 and published in 2024, gives Radon criteria for algebras generated by a nuclear space. These results address specified hypotheses, not every algebra in the notebook.',status=r'there are precise positive results in nuclear and moment-determinate settings. No exact single axiomatization intended in the notebook has been identified, and this entry does not assert that every positive functional on every Fr\'echet algebra is a moment functional.',literal='not determinable',match='partial',problem='GP-1967-20',worth=r'Worth comparing in a specified nuclear Fr\'echet class, because continuity, positivity on squares and positivity on all nonnegative functions are different conditions.',refs=['schmudgen2018moment','infusino2024moment'],confidence='medium')
i(142,'P000229','remark','Convolution algebra and Bochner representation',r'The example is \(L^1\) with convolution and \(x^*(t)=\overline{x(-t)}\), followed by the Fourier integral of a positive measure.',r'For the additive group \(\mathbb R\), a bounded continuous positive-definite function \(\varphi\) is the Fourier transform \(\varphi(t)=\int_{\mathbb R}e^{it\lambda}\,d\mu(\lambda)\) of a unique finite positive measure. It defines a bounded positive functional on \(L^1(\mathbb R)\) by \(L(f)=\int f(t)\varphi(t)\,dt\), up to the chosen Fourier-sign convention.',r'The notebook does not distinguish the function being transformed from the argument of the functional; the two roles are separated here.',fate=r'The notes identify this as Bochner\'s already known theorem. It supplies a concrete spectral model for the preceding moment question, rather than an additional generalization.',status=r'the example is standard and does not settle the countably normed case.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='none found',verification='indirect',refs=[],confidence='high')
p('GP-1967-20','Moment representations for positive functionals on countably normed algebras',r'Under which additional topological and positivity hypotheses does every continuous positive functional on a commutative unital Fr\'echet \(*\)-algebra admit a Radon measure representation on its continuous Hermitian character space?',['1967-142.3'],r'November 2026: precise subclasses and criteria are known, including nuclear-space generated algebras. The notebook\'s intended universal axiomatization is under-specified; no claim of a uniquely identified open conjecture is made.', ['schmudgen2018moment','infusino2024moment'])
save()

# 143.
t(143,'1968-02-12','I. N. Bernstein','P000228-P000233','Grothendieck topologies and sheaf cohomology',r'The substantive text is P000229--P000231. It introduces fibre products, covering axioms, presheaves, sheaves, the augmented \v{C}ech complex and derived-functor cohomology. The group example is only announced. This anticipates Geronimus\'s September exposition of Mumford, whose 1965 text already presents this machinery.')
mr=['mumford1965picard']
i(143,'P000229','definition','Fibre product by its universal property',r'An object \(V=V_1\times_U V_2\) is drawn with commuting projections and a unique factorization from every test object.',r'Given \(V_1\to U\leftarrow V_2\), the fibre product represents the functor \(W\mapsto\operatorname{Hom}(W,V_1)\times_{\operatorname{Hom}(W,U)}\operatorname{Hom}(W,V_2)\). It is unique up to unique isomorphism, when it exists, and depends on the two morphisms to \(U\), not only on the three objects.',fate=r'Expository category theory; compare \citet[p.~38, footnote]{mumford1965picard}.',status=r'this is the standard definition.',refs=mr)
i(143,'P000229-P000230','definition','Grothendieck pretopology',r'The notebook specifies a category, distinguished covering families, fibre products, identity covers, composition and base change.',r'A pretopology is a class of families \(\{U_i\to U\}\) containing isomorphisms as covers, stable under composition of covering families and under pullback along every morphism, whenever the required pullbacks exist. It generates a Grothendieck topology of covering sieves.',r'``Pretopology'' distinguishes these covering-family axioms from the equivalent sieve-based formulation; it does not change the notes\' content.',fate=r'Already standard; \citet[p.~38]{mumford1965picard}.',status=r'the definition remains in use.',refs=mr)
i(143,'P000230','construction','Local-homeomorphism site of a space',r'For an ordinary topological space, objects are spaces locally homeomorphic to it; coverings are surjective families.',r'For a space \(X\), take local homeomorphisms \(U\to X\), maps over \(X\), and jointly surjective families as covers. Fibre products over \(X\) exist and remain local homeomorphisms. Ordinary open subsets form a basis of this site, yielding the same category of sheaves as the usual topology of \(X\).',r'The structural map to the fixed base \(X\) and joint surjectivity are made explicit.',fate=r'Expository enlargement of ordinary topology; \citet[\S1 and pp.~44--45]{mumford1965picard}.',status=r'the comparison is standard.',refs=mr)
i(143,'P000230','remark','Fibre products of circular coverings',r'The circular example says \(U\times_U U=U\) and compares coverings of degrees two and three.',r'If both maps \(S^1\to S^1\) have degree two, their pullback is the disjoint union of two circles, each mapping by degree two to either factor. For degrees \(m,n>0\), it has \(\gcd(m,n)\) components and each component covers the common base with degree \(\operatorname{lcm}(m,n)\). In particular degrees two and three give one degree-six covering.',r'The equation \(U\times_U U\cong U\) is valid for identity structure maps; it is false for two copies of the degree-two cover suggested by the drawing. The unclear arrow labels are not silently repaired.',fate=r'The corrected calculation follows directly from the pullback definition: pairs satisfy \(z^m=w^n\). It is an elementary emendation, not later research.',status=r'the mathematical alternatives are settled, but the exact unlabelled arrows in the drawing remain ambiguous.',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='indirect',refs=[])
i(143,'P000230-P000231','definition','Presheaves and the sheaf equalizer condition',r'A presheaf assigns abelian groups contravariantly; compatible local sections must glue uniquely for a sheaf.',r'An abelian presheaf on a site \(\mathcal C\) is a functor \(F:\mathcal C^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Ab}\). It is a sheaf when, for every cover, \(F(U)\to\prod_iF(U_i)\rightrightarrows\prod_{i,j}F(U_i\times_UU_j)\) is an equalizer. This combines uniqueness and existence of descent.',fate=r'Expository; \citet[p.~39]{mumford1965picard}.',status=r'this is the standard sheaf axiom.',refs=mr)
i(143,'P000231','construction','Augmented Cech complex',r'The groups are indexed \(C^0=P(U), C^1=\prod P(U_i), C^2=\prod P(U_i\times_UU_j)\), with alternating restrictions.',r'The augmented complex is \(F(U)\to\check C^0(\mathcal U,F)\to\check C^1(\mathcal U,F)\to\cdots\), where \(\check C^r=\prod_{i_0,\ldots,i_r}F(U_{i_0}\times_U\cdots\times_UU_{i_r})\) and the differential is the alternating sum of omitted-factor pullbacks. A sheaf makes the first two terms exact; higher exactness needs an acyclic cover or another additional hypothesis.',r'The notebook shifts the augmented complex by one compared with common modern \v{C}ech notation. No assertion that every cover computes derived cohomology is added.',fate=r'The construction belongs to the pre-existing sheaf formalism; \citet[\S1]{mumford1965picard}.',status=r'the indexing convention is harmless when stated.',refs=mr)
i(143,'P000231','method','Derived-functor cohomology of a site',r'Global sections are left exact and their derived functors define cohomology.',r'For a site with terminal object \(e\), \(\Gamma(F)=F(e)\) on abelian sheaves is left exact. The category has enough injectives, and \(H^q(\mathcal C,F)=R^q\Gamma(F)\). Without a representable terminal object one uses the terminal sheaf to define global sections.',r'The existence of enough injectives and the terminal-object convention are supplied.',fate=r'Expository; \citet[pp.~37,39]{mumford1965picard}; see also \citet[\S\S2--3]{grothendieck1957homological}.',status=r'the definition is established.',refs=['mumford1965picard','grothendieck1957homological'])
i(143,'P000231','remark','An announced finite-group example',r'The last line announces a topology associated with representations of a finite group, without giving its objects or covers.',r'No precise theorem is recoverable from this line alone. A natural later comparison is the site of \(\Gamma\)-sets with jointly surjective covers, whose abelian sheaves are \(\Gamma\)-modules; this is explicitly constructed in record 151.',r'The \(\Gamma\)-set interpretation is a cross-reference, not a replacement for the missing February construction.',cls='F',fate=r'Compare \citet[pp.~39--41]{mumford1965picard} and occurrences 1968-151.2--1968-151.4; the isolated phrase does not establish that this was the exact category intended.',status=r'the later example is identified, while this announcement remains incomplete.',literal='not determinable',match='partial',refs=mr,confidence='medium')
c('P000230','circle-covering pullback','U times_U U = U','Retain the arrow ambiguity; for two degree-two covers the pullback has two circular components.','Scan shows repeated U and circular coverings but no unambiguous structural-map labels; direct pullback computation.','both','ambiguity')
save()

# 144.
t(144,'1968-03-11','I. M. Gelfand; A. A. Kirillov','P000231-P000234','Lie fields and virtual Laplace operators',r'P000232--P000233 develop the birational study of enveloping algebras via differential operators on the basic affine space. A central extension by virtual Laplace operators is asserted to have a Weyl division ring. This is the close precursor of the 1969 Gelfand--Kirillov paper, whereas the unextended general conjecture must be separated from it. The talk ends with questions about division-ring extensions and global holomorphic differential operators.')
gk=['gelfandkirillov1966corps'];gk69=['gelfandkirillov1969structure']
i(144,'P000232','definition','Enveloping algebra, centre and differential-operator realization',r'\(U(\mathfrak g)\) is generated by \(e_i\) with \([e_i,e_j]=\sum c_{ij}^ke_k\); central elements are called Laplace or Casimir operators. A group action yields differential operators, faithfully on the group itself.',r'For a finite-dimensional complex Lie algebra \(\mathfrak g\), \(U(\mathfrak g)=T(\mathfrak g)/(xy-yx-[x,y])\). If a Lie group with this Lie algebra acts smoothly or algebraically, differentiation gives a representation by differential operators, which may have a kernel. The representation by invariant differential operators on the group is faithful. For connected \(G\), the image of \(Z(U(\mathfrak g))\) commutes with the differentiated and group actions.',r'Connectedness is relevant to passage from infinitesimal to group invariance. PBW supplies the faithfulness assertion and the filtration underlying the later fractions.',fate=r'Background recalled in \citet[Introduction]{gelfandkirillov1966corps} and \citet[\S\S2--3]{gelfandkirillov1969structure}.',status=r'these are standard algebraic constructions.',refs=gk+gk69)
i(144,'P000232','theorem','Faithfulness on the basic affine space',r'The action of \(U(\mathfrak g)\) on \(N_-\backslash G\) is said to be faithful.',r'Let \(G\) be a simply connected complex semisimple algebraic group and \(N_-\) a maximal unipotent subgroup. Right translation on \(\mathcal O(N_-\backslash G)\) differentiates to an injective homomorphism \(U(\mathfrak g)\to\mathcal D(N_-\backslash G)\). The coordinate ring contains every finite-dimensional irreducible representation, and these representations separate elements of \(U(\mathfrak g)\).',r'The algebraic category, regular functions and simply connected group are specified. ``Nilpotent group'' is replaced by the intended maximal unipotent subgroup.',fate=r'It is part of the representation-theoretic setup in \citet[\S\S3--6]{gelfandkirillov1969structure}, not the new fraction-field theorem.',status=r'the statement is established.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',refs=gk69)
i(144,'P000232','remark','Finite-translation functions and the compact-group completeness theorem',r'A function is called regular when its translates span a finite-dimensional space; on a compact group such functions approximate all functions. The sine function on a line is an example.',r'On a compact group, finite sums of matrix coefficients of continuous finite-dimensional representations are uniformly dense in continuous functions. For a complex semisimple algebraic group, regular functions are finite sums of algebraic matrix coefficients. The finite-translation characterization must be understood with the relevant continuity, holomorphicity or algebraicity conditions; on a real additive line, sine is representative but not a polynomial regular function.',r'The notebook uses ``regular'' across analytic and algebraic categories. These two meanings are distinguished.',fate=r'Classical representation-theoretic background; the algebraic formulation is recalled in \citet[\S3]{gelfandkirillov1969structure}.',status=r'the density and algebraic matrix-coefficient statements are established in their specified categories.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',refs=gk69)
i(144,'P000232','construction','Affinization and rationality of the basic affine space',r'Take the invariant ring of functions \(f(n_-g)=f(g)\), its spectrum \(X\), and note that \(N_-\backslash G\) is birational to projective space.',r'The basic affine space \(A=N_-\backslash G\) is quasi-affine; \(X=\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O(G)^{N_-}\) is its affinization. The big Bruhat cell gives a dense open subset of \(A\) isomorphic to \(T\times N_+\), hence its function field is purely transcendental of degree \(\operatorname{rank}G+|\Phi^+|\). It is consequently rational, as is \(X\).',r'The quasi-affine quotient is not identified pointwise with its affine completion. ``Birational to projective space'' means rationality, not compactness.',fate=r'Explicit setup in \citet[\S\S3--4]{gelfandkirillov1969structure}.',status=r'this construction and rationality are established.',refs=gk69)
i(144,'P000232','construction','Ore fractions of an enveloping algebra',r'The notes stress the nontrivial existence of a division ring of quotients and that single fractions \(QP^{-1}\) suffice.',r'PBW implies that \(U(\mathfrak g)\) is a left and right Noetherian domain. It satisfies both Ore conditions and embeds in its classical division ring \(D(\mathfrak g)\). Each element can be represented as a single right fraction \(QP^{-1}\), and also as a single left fraction, with nonzero denominator.',r'The Ore condition, rather than formal commutative fraction manipulation, justifies the two-step description.',fate=r'This repeats the theme of 1965-80.1--1965-80.3; see \citet[\S1]{gelfandkirillov1966corps}.',status=r'the construction is standard for finite-dimensional Lie algebras in characteristic zero.',refs=gk)
i(144,'P000232-P000233','definition','Weyl division rings with central parameters',r'\(D_{n,k}\) uses \(x_i,\partial/\partial x_i\) and commuting parameters \(y_1,\ldots,y_k\); the two integers are invariants.',r'Over \(\mathbb C\), define \(D_{n,k}=\operatorname{Frac}(A_n(\mathbb C)\otimes\mathbb C[y_1,\ldots,y_k])\), with \([\partial_i,x_j]=\delta_{ij}\) and all other generator commutators zero. Its centre is \(\mathbb C(y_1,\ldots,y_k)\). For a semisimple Lie algebra the proposed parameters are \(k=\operatorname{rank}\mathfrak g\), \(n=|\Phi^+|\), so \(2n+k=\dim\mathfrak g\).',r'The field and algebraic independence of central parameters are stated explicitly.',fate=r'Defined and its numerical invariants studied in \citet[\S\S1,4]{gelfandkirillov1966corps}; compare 1965-80.5--1965-80.6.',status=r'the standard division rings and the distinction of their parameters are established.',refs=gk)
i(144,'P000233','conjecture','The unextended Gelfand--Kirillov conjecture for semisimple algebras',r'The conjectural isomorphism \(D(\mathfrak g)\simeq D_{n,k}\) is described as almost proved; a later parenthesis says that it is unknown for \(U\), except the unimodular case.',r'For a complex semisimple Lie algebra \(\mathfrak g\), ask whether \(\operatorname{Frac}U(\mathfrak g)\cong D_{|\Phi^+|,\operatorname{rank}\mathfrak g}\). This assertion concerns the original division ring, with no finite extension of its centre.',r'The historical term unimodular is read in its historical matrix-group sense \(SL_m\), not as the modern property \(\operatorname{tr}(\operatorname{ad}x)=0\), which every semisimple algebra has.',cls='B',fate=r'Type \(A\) was proved in \citet[\S3]{gelfandkirillov1966corps}. \citet[Theorem~1]{premet2010modular}, circulated in July 2009 and printed in 2010, disproved the conjecture for simple types \(B_\ell\; (\ell\ge3),D_\ell\;(\ell\ge4),E_6,E_7,E_8,F_4\).',status=r'the blanket semisimple assertion is false. The cited 2013 discussion still lists types \(C\) and \(G_2\) as open; no later resolution of those cases was verified in this search. The commutative rationality theorem for \(G_2\) in \citet[Theorem~2]{anderson2013g2} is not a resolution of the division-ring conjecture.',literal='false as written',problem='GP-1967-21',refs=gk+['premet2010modular','anderson2013g2'],confidence='high')
i(144,'P000233','remark','Nilpotent and algebraic solvable cases',r'For nilpotent algebras the conjecture is proved; for algebraic solvable algebras the speaker says ``probably''.',r'For a finite-dimensional nilpotent Lie algebra over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero, its enveloping division ring is a Weyl division ring with central transcendental parameters, with \(k\) the index and \(2n=\dim\mathfrak g-k\). The notebook\'s assertion about all algebraic solvable algebras is a separate tentative extension.',r'The nilpotent theorem is isolated from the unproved-at-this-point solvable remark. No theorem for arbitrary nonalgebraic solvable Lie algebras is inferred.',fate=r'The nilpotent case is proved in \citet[\S2]{gelfandkirillov1966corps}. The precise publication intended by the tentative solvable parenthesis was not established in the sources opened for this item.',status=r'the nilpotent assertion is established. This entry makes no comprehensive November 2026 classification claim for solvable Lie fields.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',refs=gk,confidence='medium')
i(144,'P000233','construction','Central extension by virtual Laplace operators',r'The new algebra is \(\widetilde U=U\otimes_Z\widetilde Z\); \(\widetilde Z/Z\) is called algebraic with Weyl Galois group.',r'For a split semisimple Lie algebra in characteristic zero, the Harish-Chandra description identifies the centre with Weyl-invariant polynomials on a Cartan dual, using the shifted Weyl action. Adjoining the Cartan coordinate functions gives a finite central algebra extension \(\widetilde U\). On fraction fields the induced extension \(\mathbb C(\mathfrak h^*)/\mathbb C(\mathfrak h^*)^W\) is Galois with group \(W\). These added commuting coordinates are the virtual Laplace operators.',r'Galois is asserted for fraction fields; the extension of rings can ramify. The shift convention is part of the Harish-Chandra identification.',cls='A',fate=r'This construction is developed in \citet[\S\S7--8]{gelfandkirillov1969structure}; the paper was received on 30 September 1968 and appeared in 1969.',status=r'the construction is established and remains distinct from the original enveloping algebra.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',refs=gk69)
i(144,'P000233','theorem','The Weyl fraction field after central extension',r'The theorem explicitly states that the fraction division ring of \(\widetilde U(\mathfrak g)\) is \(D_{n,k}\).',r'With \(\widetilde U\) the virtual-Laplace extension above, \(\operatorname{Frac}\widetilde U(\mathfrak g)\cong D_{n,k}\), where \(n=|\Phi^+|\) and \(k=\operatorname{rank}\mathfrak g\). The isomorphism is a statement after the prescribed finite extension of the centre.',r'The central extension is retained in both the hypothesis and conclusion.',cls='A',fate=r'Exact publication match: \citet[\S13, Theorem~3]{gelfandkirillov1969structure}, received September 1968 and published in 1969.',status=r'this theorem is established; its truth does not imply the unextended conjecture, which fails in several types.',refs=gk69)
i(144,'P000233','question','Algebraic extensions of Weyl division rings',r'\ru{Алг. расшир.} of \(D_{n,k}\) are queried, with algebraic varieties mentioned as the commutative analogy.',r'The note asks for a geometric or structural understanding of extensions of \(D_{n,k}\), but specifies neither finite left/right degree, centrality, embeddings, nor the equivalence relation on extensions. These choices lead to different mathematical problems, so no unique classification assertion can be reconstructed.',r'The preceding central Galois extension is an example, not a definition of every extension meant by the question.',cls='F',fate=r'\citet[Introduction and \S13]{gelfandkirillov1969structure} explains the motivating finite central extension. A precise general extension problem matching this one-line question was not identified.',status=r'the remark remains under-specified; no purported solution of noncommutative birational geometry is assigned to it.',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',refs=gk69,confidence='low')
i(144,'P000233','question','Global holomorphic differential operators on compact complex manifolds',r'The question asks to describe regular differential operators on compact complex manifolds, followed by three vector fields on the Riemann sphere.',r'For a compact complex manifold \(X\), study the filtered algebra \(H^0(X,\mathcal D_X)\), where \(\mathcal D_X\) consists of finite-order holomorphic differential operators acting on \(\mathcal O_X\). Its order-one part contains \(H^0(X,T_X)\) and constants; higher symbols lie in \(H^0(X,\operatorname{Sym}^m T_X)\), but not every symbol necessarily lifts.',r'This fixes ``regular'' as holomorphic and the coefficient bundle as trivial. The notebook gives no manifold class or requested invariant, so a universal classification target cannot be extracted.',cls='F',fate=r'The differential-operator viewpoint is explained in \citet[Introduction]{gelfandkirillov1966corps}; its basic affine-space results do not answer this compact-manifold question.',status=r'the object is meaningful, but the requested general classification is not precise enough to assign a solved/open theorem.',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',refs=gk,confidence='medium')
i(144,'P000233','example' if False else 'remark','Global vector fields on the projective line',r'The sphere has \(\partial_z,z\partial_z,z^2\partial_z\).',r'Every global holomorphic vector field on \(\mathbb P^1\) has the form \((a+bz+cz^2)\partial_z\). In the coordinate \(w=1/z\), this becomes \(-(aw^2+bw+c)\partial_w\), which is regular at infinity. The three displayed fields form a basis of \(H^0(\mathbb P^1,T_{\mathbb P^1})\cong\mathfrak{sl}_2\).',r'The observation describes order-one operators; it does not itself classify all higher-order operators on all compact manifolds.',fate=r'This is the elementary sphere example in the notes. The coordinate calculation directly verifies its global regularity.',status=r'the example is correct and established.',match='none found',verification='indirect',refs=[])
p('GP-1967-21','The unextended semisimple Gelfand--Kirillov conjecture',r'For a complex semisimple Lie algebra \(\mathfrak g\), is \(\operatorname{Frac}U(\mathfrak g)\) isomorphic to \(D_{|\Phi^+|,\operatorname{rank}\mathfrak g}\) without extending the centre?',['1967-144.7'],r'November 2026: false in general by Premet (2009 preprint; 2010 publication), true for type A. The cited 2013 source leaves C and G2 open; no later exact resolution was verified. The 1969 theorem after central extension is a different assertion.',gk+gk69+['premet2010modular','anderson2013g2'])
c('P000233','semisimple Lie field','For semisimple groups D(g) is almost proved to be D(n,k).','Retain as historical conjectural optimism; do not turn it into an established theorem.','The next notebook theorem concerns tilde U, and Premet2010 disproves the unextended blanket assertion.')
c('P000233','Galois central extension','tilde Z is an algebraic extension, Galois group Weyl group','The induced extension of fraction fields is Galois; the polynomial-ring extension may ramify.','Gelfand-Kirillov1969 §§7–8 and invariant-field interpretation.')
