# 145.
t(145,'1968-03-11','L. G. Makar-Limanov','P000232-P000235','Dixmier on the first Weyl algebra',r'The actual discussion starts near the bottom of P000233 and ends before Palamodov\'s heading on P000234. It reports Dixmier\'s automorphism theorem, strictly semisimple elements and nonpolynomial centralizers. Arnold inserts a Poisson-bracket comparison. The open division-ring automorphism question repeats GP-1965-08 and is kept separate from the algebra automorphism theorem.')
dix=['dixmier1968weyl']
i(145,'P000233','definition','The first Weyl algebra',r'The ring of quantum mechanics is generated by \(p,q\) with \([p,q]=1\).',r'\(A_1(\mathbb C)=\mathbb C\langle p,q\rangle/(pq-qp-1)\), represented faithfully by \(p=d/dx\) and \(q=x\) on \(\mathbb C[x]\). Its monomials \(p^a q^b\), \(a,b\ge0\), form a vector-space basis. Its Ore fraction division ring is \(D_1\).',r'The commutator convention is fixed and maintained in the later eigenvalue calculation.',fate=r'The notation and algebra are those of \citet[\S1]{dixmier1968weyl}; compare 1965-80.5.',status=r'the construction is standard.',refs=dix)
i(145,'P000233','theorem','Polynomial shears generate the automorphism group of A1',r'The automorphisms are generated by \(p\mapsto p, q\mapsto q+f(p)\) and \(q\mapsto q,p\mapsto p+g(q)\), or by one shear family and Fourier exchange.',r'Over \(\mathbb C\), \(\operatorname{Aut}_{\mathbb C}A_1\) is generated by the two polynomial shear families \((p,q)\mapsto(p,q+f(p))\), \((p,q)\mapsto(p+g(q),q)\). Equivalently, one may use \((p,q)\mapsto(p+g(q),q)\) together with \((p,q)\mapsto(-q,p)\); linear shears generate the required determinant-one linear transformations.',r'The statement is for automorphisms, not arbitrary endomorphisms. It does not assert the Dixmier endomorphism conjecture.',cls='E',fate=r'This is explicitly an exposition of Dixmier\'s work, published in 1968: \citet[Theorem~8.10]{dixmier1968weyl}. The notebook supplies evidence that it circulated by 11 March 1968.',status=r'the generation theorem is established.',refs=dix)
i(145,'P000233','question','Automorphisms of the Weyl division ring',r'For \(D_{1,0}\), the analogous automorphism description is unknown.',r'Describe \(\operatorname{Aut}_{\mathbb C}D_1\), where \(D_1=\operatorname{Frac}A_1(\mathbb C)\). The subgroup stabilizing the rational function subfield \(\mathbb C(q)\) consists exactly of maps \(q\mapsto\theta(q)=(aq+b)/(cq+d)\), \(p\mapsto\theta^{\prime}(q)^{-1}p+r(q)\), where \(ad-bc\ne0\) and \(r\in\mathbb C(q)\). This subgroup does not exhaust the full problem.',r'The stabilizer formula is a precisely bounded modern result, not part of the notebook\'s assertion of ignorance.',cls='C',fate=r'This repeats 1965-80.10 and retains GP-1965-08. \citet[\S5.2.4 and \S6.2.4]{dumas2011invariants} records the stabilizer and the wider unsolved automorphism problem.',status=r'the complete automorphism group is not determined by the cited results. An elementary centralizer/stabilizer argument gives a useful bounded reduction, while the full classification remains explicitly unresolved in this audit.',literal='correct',match='partial',problem='GP-1965-08',worth=r'Worth developing the rational-subfield stabilizer and its centralizer proof, because this gives a checkable reduction without claiming to classify all division-ring automorphisms.',refs=['dumas2011invariants'],confidence='high')
i(145,'P000234','remark','Arnold: polynomial canonical maps and the Poisson bracket',r'Arnold writes the usual two-variable Poisson bracket and considers polynomials \(P,Q\) for which it equals one.',r'On \(\mathbb C^2\) with coordinates \(p,q\), \(\{P,Q\}=P_pQ_q-P_qQ_p\). If \(\{P,Q\}=1\), the polynomial map \((p,q)\mapsto(P,Q)\) has Jacobian determinant one and is locally biholomorphic and symplectic. If it is additionally a polynomial automorphism, it is a global polynomial canonical transformation.',r'The distinction between local invertibility and a global polynomial inverse is essential. The notes do not state or solve the Jacobian conjecture.',fate=r'The bracket calculation is the classical analogue offered in the seminar; \citet[Introduction and \S8]{dixmier1968weyl} provides the Weyl-algebra comparison.',status=r'the local conclusion follows directly from the inverse-function theorem; no global-inverse claim is warranted.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='thematic only',refs=dix,speaker='V. I. Arnold')
i(145,'P000234','remark','The qualified relationship with associated graded Poisson algebras',r'The note says there is no connection, even with grading.',r'For the Bernstein filtration \(\deg p=\deg q=1\), \(\operatorname{gr}A_1\cong\mathbb C[p,q]\), and the commutator induces its canonical Poisson bracket of degree \(-2\). A general polynomial shear need not preserve this filtration, so taking an associated graded map does not directly compare all automorphisms.',r'This distinguishes a valid warning about arbitrary automorphisms from the overly broad denial of any graded relationship.',fate=r'The filtered construction follows from the relation \([p,q]=1\); the Newton/degree analysis in \citet[\S\S2--3]{dixmier1968weyl} is relevant background.',status=r'the blanket literal statement is false; the more restricted warning is correct.',literal='false as written',match='partial',refs=dix,confidence='medium')
i(145,'P000234','theorem','Strictly semisimple elements and the missing affine parameters',r'An element is completely semisimple when its adjoint has a complete eigenbasis; it is then said to be carried to \(pq\).',r'For a nonscalar \(x\in A_1(\mathbb C)\) such that \(\operatorname{ad}x\) is diagonalizable on the whole algebra, there are an automorphism \(\alpha\), a nonzero \(a\in\mathbb C\), and \(b\in\mathbb C\) with \(\alpha(x)=a pq+b\). The coefficient and scalar term cannot generally be removed by an automorphism.',r'``Complete system of eigenvalues'' is read as an eigenvector decomposition. Scalars are excluded from the strictly semisimple case.',fate=r'Exact corrected form: \citet[Theorem~9.2 and Corollary~9.3]{dixmier1968weyl}.',status=r'the corrected theorem is established; the notebook\'s single normal form \(pq\) is too restrictive.',literal='false as written',refs=dix)
i(145,'P000234','claim','Eigenvalues of the Euler element under the stated commutator convention',r'The formula gives \(p^a q^b\mapsto(a-b)p^a q^b\).',r'With \([p,q]=1\), \([pq,p]=-p\), \([pq,q]=q\), hence \([pq,p^a q^b]=(b-a)p^a q^b\). Thus the Weyl monomials are eigenvectors of \(\operatorname{ad}(pq)\), with eigenvalues \(b-a\).',r'The sign is fixed by the preceding definition, not by a different convention silently substituted for it.',fate=r'Direct commutator computation confirms the correction; compare \citet[\S5.3, equation~(4)]{dixmier1968weyl}.',status=r'the printed \(a-b\) has the opposite sign under the notebook\'s convention.',literal='false as written',refs=dix)
i(145,'P000234','theorem','Commutative centralizers of nonscalar Weyl elements',r'A result attributed to Amitsur says the centralizer \(C(x)\) is commutative.',r'For every nonscalar \(x\in A_1(\mathbb C)\), \(C_{A_1}(x)\) is commutative. It is a finitely generated commutative algebra of transcendence degree one. For a scalar \(x\), its centralizer is all of \(A_1\), so that exception is necessary.',r'The nonscalar hypothesis and the precise ambient algebra are supplied; the note does not claim this for every arbitrary noncommutative algebra.',fate=r'\citet[\S4.2 and Theorem~5.1]{dixmier1968weyl} states the centralizer results and credits Amitsur for commutativity.',status=r'the nonscalar theorem is established.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',refs=dix)
i(145,'P000234','construction','Elliptic and cuspidal centralizers',r'The notes say centralizers need not be polynomial rings and display \(X^3=Y^2+\alpha\) for arbitrary \(\alpha\in\mathbb C\).',r'There are commuting elements \(X,Y\in A_1(\mathbb C)\) satisfying \(Y^2=X^3-\alpha\), whose generated commutative algebra has that sole defining relation and occurs as a centralizer in Dixmier\'s construction. For \(\alpha\ne0\) this is the affine coordinate ring of a nonsingular elliptic curve with one point removed; for \(\alpha=0\) it is a cuspidal cubic ring, not \(\mathbb C[t]\).',r'Commutativity and absence of additional algebraic relations are needed for the geometric conclusion; the displayed equation by itself would not prove it.',fate=r'Exact family in \citet[\S\S5.5--5.6]{dixmier1968weyl}.',status=r'the examples are established and refute the assertion that every nonscalar centralizer is a polynomial ring.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',refs=dix)
p('GP-1965-08','Automorphisms of Weyl division rings',r'Describe the automorphism groups of Weyl division rings; in the present occurrence, determine \(\operatorname{Aut}_{\mathbb C}\operatorname{Frac}A_1(\mathbb C)\), keeping polynomial algebra automorphisms and rational-subfield stabilizers distinct.',['1965-80.10','1967-145.3'],r'November 2026: the full group remains unresolved in the verified literature. The stabilizer of C(q) is explicitly described by rational shears and lifted fractional-linear transformations; this is a bounded result, not a complete classification.',['dumas2011invariants'])
c('P000234','strictly semisimple normal form','x is carried by an automorphism to pq','x is carried to a pq+b, a nonzero; exclude scalar x.','Scan confirms pq without parameters; Dixmier1968 Theorem9.2 and Corollary9.3.')
c('P000234','Euler eigenvalue','[pq,p^a q^b]=(a-b)p^a q^b','[pq,p^a q^b]=(b-a)p^a q^b for [p,q]=1.','Scan confirms a-b; direct computation gives [pq,p]=-p and [pq,q]=q.')
c('P000234','Amitsur centralizer','C(x) is commutative','C(x) is commutative for nonscalar x in A1.','Scalar x has centralizer A1; Dixmier1968 §4.2.')
c('P000234','Poisson comparison','No connection, even with grading','There is an associated graded Poisson algebra, but arbitrary automorphisms need not preserve the chosen filtration.','PBW and the Bernstein filtration; retain the notebook claim as a qualified historical remark.')
save()

# 146.
t(146,'1968-03-11','V. P. Palamodov','P000233-P000236','Hormander: weighted d-bar existence and approximation',r"""The discussion on P000234--P000235 before the heading 18/III reports H\"ormander's \(L^2\) existence and approximation theory. The estimate has a recognizable 1965 source. The growth-class approximation and Martineau indicator remarks are more compressed; their weight conventions and limiting operations require separate scrutiny.""")
hr=['hormander1965l2']
i(146,'P000234','definition','Weighted Dolbeault spaces and plurisubharmonic exhaustion',r"""The Levi form \(L(\phi)\) is nonnegative, and \(\|f\|_\phi^2=\int_\Omega |f|^2e^{-\phi}dV\). Compact sublevel sets are said to imply pseudoconvexity.""",r"""For an open \(\Omega\subset\mathbb C^n\) and a real \(C^2\) function \(\phi\), its Levi form is \(\sum_{j,k}\phi_{j\bar k}\xi_j\overline{\xi_k}\). Nonnegativity is equivalent to plurisubharmonicity. The weighted space \(L^2_{p,q}(\Omega,e^{-\phi})\) consists of measurable forms with finite displayed norm. A plurisubharmonic exhaustion, whose sublevels are relatively compact, makes \(\Omega\) pseudoconvex.""",r"""Exhaustion is a global hypothesis; it does not follow from nonnegativity of the Levi form alone.""",fate=r"""These are the definitions used in \citet[\S\S2.1--2.2]{hormander1965l2}.""",status='the formulation is standard.',refs=hr)
i(146,'P000234','theorem','A weighted L2 solution with logarithmic loss',r"""For \(q\ge1\), \(\bar\partial f=0\) and \(f\in L^2(\phi)\), the notes claim \(\bar\partial g=f\), \(g\in L^2(\phi+2\log(1+|z|^2))\), and \(\sqrt q\|g\|\le\|f\|\).""",r"""Let \(\Omega\subset\mathbb C^n\) be pseudoconvex, \(\phi\in C^2(\Omega)\) plurisubharmonic, \(1\le q\le n\), and let \(f\) be a distributionally \(\bar\partial\)-closed \((p,q)\)-form with \(\int |f|^2e^{-\phi}<\infty\). Then a solution \(g\) satisfies \(\int |g|^2e^{-\phi}(1+|z|^2)^{-2}\le q^{-1}\int|f|^2e^{-\phi}\).""",r"""The norm on the left uses the modified weight, while that on the right uses the original weight. With the usual Euclidean coefficient norm, the lower Levi eigenvalue of \(2\log(1+|z|^2)\) even gives the stronger factor \(1/(2q)\); the displayed weaker inequality is therefore consistent.""",fate=r"""A direct specialization of \citet[Theorems~2.2.1 and 2.2.1\(^{\prime}\)]{hormander1965l2}, published three years before the seminar.""",status='the existence theorem is established with the specified weights.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',refs=hr)
i(146,'P000235','claim','Density of entire functions with weighted growth',r"""The union of entire functions in \(L^2(\phi+c\log(1+|z|^2))\) is called nontrivial and dense in all entire functions for compact convergence; the note identifies this with a pointwise exponential-polynomial bound.""",r"""Fix \(\phi\) and define \(\mathcal A_\phi=\bigcup_{c>0}\{f\in\mathcal O(\mathbb C^n):\int|f|^2e^{-\phi}(1+|z|^2)^{-c}<\infty\}\). The recorded assertion is density of \(\mathcal A_\phi\) in \(\mathcal O(\mathbb C^n)\). Under the explicitly sufficient extra hypothesis that \(\phi\) is bounded below, every polynomial belongs to this union, so density follows from Taylor approximation. For arbitrary weights, integral and pointwise bounds require their precise regularization and factor-of-two conventions.""",r"""The bounded-below case is a verified elementary reconstruction, not asserted as the entire intended theorem. The quoted pointwise bound \(e^\phi\) cannot simply be substituted for the weight \(e^{-\phi}\) in an \(L^2\) norm.""",cls='F',fate=r"""\citet[Theorem~2.3.5 and Corollary~2.3.6]{hormander1965l2} gives a precise Runge approximation theorem, but its statement is not the identical fixed-growth-class assertion. The exact version intended here was not established from the opened source.""",status='the elementary bounded-below case is settled; the full intended growth-weight statement remains unidentified in this audit.',literal='not determinable',match='partial',refs=hr,confidence='medium')
i(146,'P000235','claim','The Martineau indicator assertion',r"""The notebook attributes to ``Martino'' the assertion that indicators are all radially homogeneous plurisubharmonic functions, with \(i(z)=\lim_{\tau\to\infty}\tau^{-k}\log|f(\tau z)|\).""",r"""For an entire function of finite order \(\rho>0\), a radial indicator is normally defined using \(h_f(z)=\limsup_{r\to\infty}r^{-\rho}\log|f(rz)|\), followed, when required, by upper-semicontinuous regularization. It is positively homogeneous of degree \(\rho\). A realization theorem must specify order, growth type, the regularized indicator and possible exceptional sets; the displayed ordinary limit need not exist.""",r"""The limsup is a mathematical reconstruction. The scan's stroke above lim is not sufficiently clear to certify a transcription correction, and ``plurisubharmonic harmonic'' in the translation must not be read as two simultaneous conditions.""",cls='F',fate=r"""The author's name strongly suggests Andr\'e Martineau. \citet[\S2.5, Theorem~2.5.2]{hormander1965l2} discusses the related P\'olya--Ehrenpreis--Martineau theorem for analytic functionals, but that is not the claimed indicator-realization theorem. No exact publication match with all of this note's hypotheses was verified.""",status='the attribution and precise realization theorem remain unresolved leads; no new unsolved problem is inferred from that bibliographic gap.',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',refs=hr,confidence='low')
c('P000235','weighted growth equivalence','L2(phi+c log(1+|z|²)) means |f|≤C exp(phi)(1+|z|²)^k','Keep the L2 and pointwise assertions separate until the weight convention and local regularization are specified.','The square in the weighted norm and mean-value estimates alter the exponent; no literal same-weight equivalence follows.')
c('P000235','Martineau indicator limit','lim r^(-k) log|f(rz)|','Retain lim/limsup ambiguity; the usual radial indicator uses limsup and often upper-semicontinuous regularization.','Scan has an unresolved stroke above lim; ordinary radial limits can fail.','both','ambiguity')
save()

# 147.
t(147,'1968-03-18','V. P. Palamodov','P000234-P000236','Fixed-degree Dolbeault cohomology and Levi conditions',r"""The continuation starts at 18/III on P000235 and occupies P000235--P000236. It treats convex weights, a one-variable growth example, fixed-degree Levi signatures, finite-dimensional cohomology, vanishing, Runge restriction, and the weighted integration-by-parts identity. A bounded-disc claim is false literally; Gelfand's short ``domains of regularity'' question is too incomplete to equate with a modern regularity conjecture.""")
i(147,'P000235','remark','Convexity of weights depending only on real parts',r"""If \(\phi=\phi(x)\), it is plurisubharmonic exactly when it is convex; the notes also recall subharmonicity on complex lines.""",r"""Let \(\phi(z)=u(\operatorname{Re}z)\), with \(u\in C^2(\mathbb R^n,\mathbb R)\). Then \(\phi_{z_j\bar z_k}=\frac14u_{x_jx_k}\); thus \(\phi\) is plurisubharmonic exactly when \(u\) is convex. More generally a continuous function is plurisubharmonic exactly when its restriction to every complex affine line is subharmonic wherever defined.""",r"""The regularity is stated for the Hessian proof; the distributional formulation extends the equivalence.""",fate=r"""This is standard background for the weights in \citet[\S2.2]{hormander1965l2}.""",status='the statement is established.',refs=hr)
i(147,'P000235','remark','Solving d-bar with exponential type and polynomial growth',r"""For \(\bar\partial u=f\) on the plane, \(|f(z)|\le C e^{a|\operatorname{Im}z|}(1+|z|^2)^k\); a solution in this growth class is called nontrivial.""",r"""For measurable \(f\) satisfying this bound, with \(a\ge0\) and finite \(k\), the weighted \(L^2\) method gives a distributional solution with the same exponential type and a possibly larger polynomial exponent. One chooses a plurisubharmonic weight comparable to \(2a|\operatorname{Im}z|+c\log(1+|z|^2)\); the logarithmic loss is absorbed by increasing \(c\).""",r"""The phrase ``this class'' is interpreted as allowing some polynomial exponent, not preserving the exact given \(k\). Interior estimates or a local Cauchy decomposition are needed to convert the weighted integral estimate to pointwise growth.""",fate=r"""This illustrates \citet[\S2.4]{hormander1965l2}, rather than a new independent theorem of Palamodov.""",status='the growth-class solvability is established with a polynomial loss; no fixed-exponent estimate is claimed.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',refs=hr)
i(147,'P000235','claim','The bounded right-hand-side assertion on the unit disc',r"""The notes say that for bounded \(f\) on the unit disc, \(\partial_z u=f\) (or \(\partial_{\bar z}u=f\)) need not have a bounded solution.""",r"""For every \(f\in L^\infty(\mathbb D)\), \(u(z)=\pi^{-1}\int_{\mathbb D}f(\zeta)/(z-\zeta)\,dA(\zeta)\) solves \(\bar\partial u=f\) distributionally and satisfies \(\|u\|_\infty\le4\|f\|_\infty\). Conjugating the kernel gives the corresponding result for \(\partial_z\). Thus the recorded nonexistence claim is false without another omitted requirement, such as a boundary condition or a different data norm.""",r"""The scan confirms the negative assertion. The denominator's bar ambiguity does not rescue it, since both operators have the same bounded-data conclusion.""",fate=r"""The correction follows from \(\bar\partial(1/(\pi z))=\delta_0\) and the bound \(\mathbb D\subset B(z,2)\), \(\pi^{-1}\int_{B(z,2)}|z-\zeta|^{-1}dA=4\). This is an elementary verification, not a later solution of a research problem.""",status='the literal assertion is refuted by the displayed solution operator.',literal='false as written',match='none found',verification='indirect',refs=[])
i(147,'P000235','remark','A measure-growth condition of Carleson type',r"""A measure \(f\) is drawn near the disc boundary with \(|f(S\cap\Omega)|\le C R_S\), said to suffice for solvability.""",r"""A standard precise growth condition would be \(|\mu|(B(\zeta,r)\cap\mathbb D)\le Cr\) for boundary-centred discs, where \(|\mu|\) is total variation. The notebook does not specify disc centres, total variation, boundary versus interior boundedness, or the solution's trace. Those choices are essential to a \(\bar\partial\) theorem with measure data.""",r"""The name ``Carleson type'' describes the scaling; it is not an exact identification with a corona theorem or a bounded-interior solution theorem.""",cls='F',fate=r"""Searches for the displayed measure condition and bounded \(\bar\partial\) solvability did not determine a unique theorem matching the drawing. The nearby \(L^2\) theorem in \citet[\S2.2]{hormander1965l2} does not by itself establish this measure-data statement.""",status='the intended norm and measure theorem remain unidentified.',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',refs=hr,confidence='low')
i(147,'P000235','definition','The boundary signature condition aq',r"""On the complex tangent hyperplane, the Levi form has at least \(q+1\) negative or \(n-q\) positive eigenvalues.""",r"""For a defining function \(\rho\) with \(d\rho\ne0\) on \(\partial\Omega\), condition \(a_q\) requires that the Hermitian form \(\partial\bar\partial\rho\) restricted to \(\ker\partial\rho\) have at least \(q+1\) strictly negative eigenvalues or at least \(n-q\) strictly positive eigenvalues at each boundary point, with \(\Omega=\{\rho<0\}\).""",r"""The normal orientation and strictness are fixed. The complex tangent space has dimension \(n-1\).""",fate=r"""Exact definition in \citet[Definition~3.3.4]{hormander1965l2}.""",status='the condition is established terminology for the ensuing estimates.',refs=hr)
i(147,'P000235','claim','Pseudoconvexity and the incorrect index a0',r"""The boundary is called pseudoconvex if \(a_0\) holds, glossed as \(n-1\) positive eigenvalues.""",r"""A \(C^2\) boundary is Levi pseudoconvex if the tangential Levi form is nonnegative. Strict pseudoconvexity means all \(n-1\) tangential eigenvalues are positive; this is the positive branch of \(a_1\), and it implies \(a_q\) for \(q\ge1\). It is not the positive branch of \(a_0\), which would demand \(n\) positive eigenvalues on an \((n-1)\)-dimensional space.""",r"""Both the index and the distinction between weak and strict pseudoconvexity must be corrected.""",fate=r"""Compare \citet[Definition~3.3.4 and Theorem~3.5.1]{hormander1965l2}; the notebook's index is inconsistent with its immediately preceding definition.""",status='the literal identification is false; the corrected strict-pseudoconvex statement is standard.',literal='false as written',refs=hr)
i(147,'P000235','theorem','Finite-dimensional weighted cohomology and closed range',r"""For a bounded domain with \(C^3\) boundary satisfying \(a_q\), the cohomology is finite dimensional and the image is closed.""",r"""Let \(\Omega\) be relatively compact in a Hermitian complex \(n\)-manifold, with \(C^3\) boundary satisfying \(a_q\), and let the real weight be continuous on \(\overline\Omega\). For the maximal weak \(\bar\partial\) operators in the \((p,q-1),(p,q),(p,q+1)\) weighted \(L^2\) spaces, \(\operatorname{im}\bar\partial\) at degree \(q\) is closed, and \(\ker\bar\partial/\operatorname{im}\bar\partial\) at degree \(q\) is finite dimensional.""",r"""The Hilbert-space domains and the weight's boundary control are supplied; arbitrary singular weights are not covered by this statement.""",fate=r"""Exact match with \citet[Theorem~3.4.1]{hormander1965l2}.""",status='the theorem is established under these boundary and weight hypotheses.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',refs=hr)
i(147,'P000235','question','Gelfand asks about domains of regularity',r"""\ru{Гельфанд: области регулярности --- ?} Palamodov reportedly says that nobody is working on this.""",r"""The phrase does not identify a function space, operator domain, boundary regularity scale or necessary-and-sufficient condition. It could concern solvability domains or regularity of solutions, but no unique mathematical question can be recovered.""",r"""No identification with later global regularity of the \(\bar\partial\)-Neumann problem, or with a particular counterexample, is made.""",cls='F',fate=r"""The surrounding lecture has the definite scope of \citet[\S3.4]{hormander1965l2}; its literature does not determine which additional question Gelfand meant. The historical report about other researchers is not accepted as a verified priority claim.""",status='the intended question remains unrecoverable from this sentence.',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',refs=hr,confidence='low',speaker='I. M. Gelfand')
i(147,'P000235-P000236','theorem','Global exhaustion and vanishing in a fixed degree',r"""A real function \(\Psi\) on the whole domain, with one more positive Levi direction, is said to force zero cohomology.""",r"""Let \(X\) be a complex \(n\)-manifold with a \(C^2\) exhaustion \(\Psi\). Assume that outside a compact sublevel its regular levels satisfy \(a_q\), and everywhere its full Levi form has at least \(q+1\) negative or at least \(n-q+1\) positive eigenvalues. Then \(H^q(X,\Omega_X^p)=0\). In the positive branch this is the familiar \(q\)-complete vanishing setting.""",r"""Proper exhaustion and the level-set hypothesis are restored. Existence of an arbitrary local function with a suitable Hessian is not enough.""",fate=r"""Exact fixed-degree form in \citet[Theorem~3.4.10]{hormander1965l2}.""",status='the theorem is established with these global hypotheses.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',refs=hr)
i(147,'P000236','remark','Andreotti--Grauert extension to complex spaces',r"""The notes attribute all these theorems for complex spaces to Andreotti and Grauert.""",r"""For a strongly \(q\)-pseudoconvex complex space \(X\) and a coherent analytic sheaf \(\mathcal F\), the relevant higher cohomology groups are finite dimensional in the prescribed degree range; for \(q\)-complete spaces, \(H^j(X,\mathcal F)=0\) for \(j\ge q\). Pseudoconcave variants have ranges involving the depth of \(\mathcal F\), so they cannot be obtained merely by copying every smooth-domain formula.""",r"""This states the positive branch explicitly and flags the additional depth requirements for the opposite branch.""",fate=r"""Already published in \citet[\S5, finiteness and vanishing theorems]{andreottigrauert1962finitude}.""",status='the coherent-sheaf results are established; the blanket phrase requires degree and depth qualifications.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',refs=['andreottigrauert1962finitude'])
i(147,'P000236','theorem','Runge restriction and extension of solvability',r"""For \(\omega=\{\Psi<0\}\subset\Omega\), with a Levi condition outside \(\omega\) and \(\Psi\to\infty\) at the boundary, cohomology restricts injectively. Solvability on \(\omega\) then gives solvability on \(\Omega\).""",r"""A precise sufficient version is: let \(X\) have a \(C^3\) exhaustion \(\Psi\) whose regular levels outside \(X_c=\{\Psi<c\}\) satisfy \(a_q\). Then the restriction \(H^q(X,\Omega_X^p)\to H^q(X_c,\Omega_X^p)\) is injective. For relatively compact smooth sublevels the analogous statement holds for the weighted \(L^2\) cohomology with weights continuous on the closure.""",r"""The condition is imposed on level-set tangent hyperplanes, not replaced without justification by a signature of the full Hessian. The strict positive full-Hessian branch in the notes implies this level condition; the negative branch requires care.""",fate=r"""\citet[Theorems~3.4.6 and 3.4.9]{hormander1965l2}.""",status='the restriction theorem is established; it is an injectivity statement, not unconditional vanishing.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',refs=hr)
i(147,'P000236','method','Weighted integration by parts and boundary Levi form',r"""The final displayed identity contains the weighted \(\bar\partial\) and adjoint norms, interior Hessian terms, derivative norms and the boundary Levi integral.""",r"""For a smooth \((0,q)\)-form \(u\) on a smooth bounded domain, satisfying the \(\bar\partial\)-Neumann boundary condition \(\sum_j\rho_{z_j}u_{jK}=0\), the Morrey--Kohn--H\"ormander identity expresses \(\|\bar\partial u\|_\phi^2+\|\bar\partial_\phi^*u\|_\phi^2\) as the sum of coefficient \(\bar z\)-derivative norms, the weighted interior Levi form \(\sum\phi_{j\bar k}u_{jK}\overline{u_{kK}}\), and the boundary Levi form \(\sum\rho_{j\bar k}u_{jK}\overline{u_{kK}}\), with boundary measure divided by \(|\nabla\rho|\) unless \(\rho\) is normalized.""",r"""The boundary condition, normalization and the weighted formal adjoint are necessary for this precise identity; the general \((p,q)\) version is coefficientwise.""",fate=r"""The proof mechanism is \citet[Proposition~2.1.2, equation~(2.1.14)]{hormander1965l2}.""",status='the identity is established and explains the role of the Levi signatures.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',refs=hr)
c('P000235','bounded disc equation','For bounded f the equation du/dz=f need not admit a bounded solution','With no extra condition, bounded data on the disc always have a bounded distributional solution; the same holds for d-bar.','Scan confirms the negative claim; the Cauchy-Green operator has L-infinity bound at most4.')
c('P000235','pseudoconvexity index','a0; n-1 positive eigenvalues','Strict pseudoconvexity gives the positive branch of a1; weak pseudoconvexity requires nonnegative eigenvalues.','Scan confirms a0; the preceding formula n-q positive makes a0 dimensionally impossible in the positive branch.')
c('P000236','boundary identity','Displayed weighted identity without explicit adjoint boundary condition','Require the d-bar-Neumann boundary condition and the defining-function normalization (or divide boundary measure by |grad rho|).','Hormander1965 Proposition2.1.2 and equation(2.1.14).')
save()

# Extra distinct assertions in the continuation, kept separate from the estimate identity.
i(147,'P000236','theorem','Approximation of closed forms across a Runge exhaustion',r"""Closed forms in \(\omega\) are approximated by closed forms in \(\Omega\).""",r"""Under the hypotheses of the preceding Runge theorem, \(\bar\partial\)-closed \((p,q-1)\)-forms on the inner sublevel are limits of restrictions of \(\bar\partial\)-closed forms on the larger domain. In the relatively compact setting the approximation is in the specified weighted \(L^2\) norm; along an exhaustion one obtains the corresponding local form of approximation.""",r"""The degree is \(q-1\), while the cohomological injection is in degree \(q\). No uniform-up-to-boundary approximation of arbitrary forms is inferred.""",fate=r"""Exact source: \citet[Theorems~3.4.7 and 3.4.9]{hormander1965l2}.""",status='the approximation theorem is established with the stated degree and norm.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',refs=hr)
i(147,'P000236','theorem','Existence on a Levi-pseudoconvex bounded domain',r"""A \(C^2\) defining function with nonnegative tangential Levi form is followed by ``solvability follows'' from the identity.""",r"""If \(\Omega\Subset\mathbb C^n\) has \(C^2\) pseudoconvex boundary and \(1\le q\le n\), every distributionally closed \(f\in L^2_{p,q}(\Omega)\) has a solution \(u\in L^2_{p,q-1}(\Omega)\), with \(\|u\|_2\le C_\Omega\|f\|_2\). One may use the strictly plurisubharmonic bounded weight \(|z|^2\), obtain a weighted estimate, and compare weighted and unweighted norms on the bounded domain.""",r"""This is solvability in \(L^2\), not smoothness of the solution up to the boundary. The notebook's comparison with grad and curl concerns the integration-by-parts mechanism.""",fate=r"""\citet[Theorem~2.2.3]{hormander1965l2}; the weighted identity is the analytic tool, not an additional regularity theorem.""",status='the stated existence result is established.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',refs=hr)
save()

# 148--149.
t(148,'','','P000237-P000238','Programme for the 1968--1969 seminar',r"""P000237 lists proposed subjects: Mumford's moduli spaces (Geronimus); Kirillov on characters; Kazhdan on Dwork; Griffiths on geometry, topology and differential equations; the Spencer resolution (Palamodov); Gromov; Buslaev on eigenvalues including Maslov's work; Grauert--Remmert on \(p\)-adic analysis with Shnirelman's earlier Cauchy-integral ideas; Nilsson; Vershik on factors of types II and III, or Sinai and \(K^0\); and H\"ormander on Lie groups and hypoellipticity. This is a programme, not eleven mathematical theorems. Gelfand's substantive remarks below it are assigned to record 149.""",'heading')
save()
t(149,'1968-09-02','I. M. Gelfand','P000237-P000239','Programme remarks: Dwork and Gromov',r"""The mathematical remarks on P000237 concern arithmetic rationality, \(p\)-adic operators, foliations and the passage from germs to jets. The rationality slogan omits decisive global hypotheses; the foliation slogan likewise cannot be treated as an unrestricted theorem. The final psychology anecdote contains no mathematical assertion and is retained only as evidence of the programme discussion. The date is supplied by the catalogue.""")
i(149,'P000237','claim','Dwork rationality and the missing global hypothesis',r"""A power series convergent near zero over the complex numbers and for all \(p\)-adic metrics is said to be rational.""",r"""Local convergence alone is insufficient: \(e^t=\sum t^n/n!\) has positive radius in \(\mathbb C\) and in every \(\mathbb Q_p\), but is not rational. A precise sufficient Dwork criterion is that \(F\in\mathbb Z[[t]]\) have positive complex radius and admit meromorphic continuation to the entire \(p\)-adic affine line for one prime \(p\); then \(F\in\mathbb Q(t)\).""",r"""Integer coefficients and global \(p\)-adic meromorphic continuation are restored. The rationality theorem is not a statement about arbitrary convergent germs at all places.""",fate=r"""\citet[Introduction and \S4]{dwork1960rationality} supplies the arithmetic-analytic argument. It was published in 1960 and available in Russian in 1961, before the seminar.""",status='the corrected criterion and rationality of finite-field zeta functions are established; the local-convergence slogan is false.',literal='false as written',match='partial',refs=['dwork1960rationality'])
i(149,'P000237','method','Zeta functions through p-adic completely continuous operators',r"""The idea is to represent the zeta function using completely continuous operators over a \(p\)-adic field.""",r"""For a variety \(X/\mathbb F_q\), \(Z(X,t)=\exp(\sum_{m\ge1}\#X(\mathbb F_{q^m})t^m/m)\). Dwork's method expresses point counts through traces of suitable \(p\)-adic operators, and the resulting Fredholm determinants give \(p\)-adic meromorphic continuation. Combined with arithmetic coefficient control and the rationality criterion, this proves \(Z(X,t)\in\mathbb Q(t)\).""",r"""A trace or Fredholm formula alone does not automatically prove rationality of an infinite-dimensional determinant.""",fate=r"""The analytic continuation and arithmetic steps are separated in \citet[\S\S3--4]{dwork1960rationality}.""",status='the method and theorem are established; no new claim is attributed to this brief programme remark.',refs=['dwork1960rationality'])
i(149,'P000237','remark','Plane distributions and the foliation existence slogan',r"""For one-dimensional foliations the question is said to reduce to vector fields; for higher codimension, existence is asserted whenever a family of planes exists.""",r"""A smooth nowhere-zero line distribution is involutive and determines a one-dimensional foliation. A higher-rank plane distribution need not be involutive. The meaningful deformation question asks whether a given distribution is homotopic through plane distributions to the tangent distribution of a foliation, under specified manifold and normal-bundle hypotheses.""",r"""The rank-one observation is exact. The higher-rank assertion lacks the hypotheses of a general theorem and cannot be read as integrability of the given distribution.""",cls='F',fate=r"""\citet[\S4.4, Theorem~4.4.2 and Corollary~4.4.3]{gromov1969stable}, received July 1968 and published in 1969, treats qualified foliation existence on open manifolds. It does not justify the unqualified notebook sentence.""",status='the broad announcement remains under-specified; Frobenius integrability and homotopy-to-foliation are distinct assertions.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',refs=['gromov1969stable'],confidence='medium')
i(149,'P000237','claim','Germs, jets and a weak homotopy equivalence',r"""The sheaf of germs and the sheaf of jets are called weakly homotopy equivalent; a global set of coefficients is said to permit construction of a function.""",r"""A precise related theorem is the open invariant relation theorem: on an open smooth manifold, for the natural bundles and open diffeomorphism-invariant differential relations of Gromov's construction, the holonomic-section map to formal solutions is a weak homotopy equivalence. This is not true for an arbitrary differential relation or an arbitrary prescribed jet section.""",r"""Open manifold, openness, invariance and the specified natural-bundle setting are necessary qualifications. A formal solution is deformed to a holonomic one; its coefficients need not be realized without deformation.""",cls='B',fate=r"""\citet[Theorem~4.1.1]{gromov1969stable}, received on 8 July 1968 and printed in 1969. Record 162 gives a fuller seminar exposition of this subject.""",status='the qualified theorem is established; the unrestricted slogan is not valid.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',refs=['gromov1969stable'])
c('P000237','arithmetic rationality','A series converging near0 complexly and at every p-adic place is rational','Local convergence does not imply rationality; restore arithmetic coefficient restrictions and global p-adic meromorphic continuation.','Scan confirms only convergence near0; exp(t) is a counterexample. Dwork1960 §4 states the genuine rationality argument.')
c('P000237','germs and jets','Germs and jets are weakly homotopy equivalent','Specify the open-manifold, open invariant-relation hypotheses and homotopy of formal data.','Gromov1969 Theorem4.1.1; the general unrestricted assertion fails.')
save()

# 150.
t(150,'1968-09-02','A. Yu. Geronimus','P000237-P000242','Mumford: moduli, universality and automorphisms',r"""P000238--P000240 introduce compact Riemann surfaces and the elliptic modular quotient, then compare a set of isomorphism classes with a universal family and a representable functor. Automorphisms produce nontrivial isotrivial families, obstructing a fine parameter space. The proposed remedy is Mumford's category and topology of families, not merely the coarse quotient. Kirillov's intervention supplies the functorial formulation.""")
i(150,'P000238','remark','Genus zero and genus one uniformization',r"""All genus-zero compact Riemann surfaces are spheres; a genus-one surface has universal cover the complex plane and a lattice of deck translations.""",r"""A connected compact Riemann surface of genus zero is biholomorphic to \(\mathbb P^1\). A connected compact genus-one surface, after choosing a point as origin, is \(E=\mathbb C/\Lambda\), where \(\Lambda=\mathbb Z\omega_1+\mathbb Z\omega_2\) is a rank-two lattice with \(\operatorname{Im}(\omega_2/\omega_1)>0\). The marked point equips it with an elliptic-curve group law.""",r"""The notebook initially discusses curves without a marked point; pointing is necessary for the elliptic group structure used later.""",fate=r"""Classical background to \citet[\S\S4,7]{mumford1965picard}.""",status='the classification is established.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',refs=mr)
i(150,'P000238','construction','Lattice homothety and the modular group action',r"""Lattices differing by nonzero complex scaling give isomorphic curves; a change of integral basis yields a fractional-linear transformation.""",r"""Pointed complex elliptic curves \(\mathbb C/\Lambda\) and \(\mathbb C/\Lambda'\) are isomorphic exactly when \(\Lambda'=a\Lambda\) for some \(a\in\mathbb C^*\). After normalization \(\Lambda_\tau=\mathbb Z+\mathbb Z\tau\), \(\operatorname{Im}\tau>0\), the basis-change group \(\Gamma=SL_2(\mathbb Z)\) acts by \(\tau\mapsto(a\tau+b)/(c\tau+d)\).""",r"""The denominator is \(c\tau+d\), as confirmed by the later formula on P000250--P000251. The P000238 denominator appears to repeat \(b\).""",fate=r"""The same presentation is used in \citet[\S7, pp.~76--78]{mumford1965picard}.""",status='the lattice criterion and action are established.',literal='false as written',refs=mr)
i(150,'P000238','construction','Fundamental region and the coarse j-line',r"""The upper half-plane modulo \(SL_2(\mathbb Z)\) is depicted by the usual modular fundamental region and called the plane of elliptic curves.""",r"""A standard closed fundamental region is \(\{\tau\in\mathbb H:|\operatorname{Re}\tau|\le1/2,\ |\tau|\ge1\}\), with the prescribed boundary identifications. The analytic coarse quotient \(\mathbb H/SL_2(\mathbb Z)\) is the complex affine line via the \(j\)-invariant. It classifies isomorphism classes of individual elliptic curves, but does not represent their family functor.""",r"""The boundary identifications and the word coarse prevent the drawing from being interpreted as a universal family.""",fate=r"""\citet[\S4 and \S7]{mumford1965picard} makes this coarse/family distinction.""",status='the coarse classification is established.',refs=mr)
i(150,'P000238-P000239','definition','A family listing isomorphism classes is not yet universal',r"""A first definition asks for a family \(X\to Y\) whose fibres are the pairwise nonisomorphic objects of a class \(K\), one of each; uniqueness is then discussed.""",r"""A family with one fibre in each isomorphism class is a set-theoretic parametrization. It need not be universal for families, need not exist with the proposed regularity, and has no categorical uniqueness merely from this condition. A moduli problem must also specify which families over each test base are admissible and how they pull back.""",r"""This preserves the progression of the lecture from a provisional definition to a functorial one.""",fate=r"""The distinction motivates \citet[Introduction and \S3]{mumford1965picard}.""",status='the provisional definition is insufficient for a fine moduli problem.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',refs=mr)
i(150,'P000239','construction','Pullback of an analytic family',r"""The fibre product construction induces a family over a new base and has a universal property.""",r"""For a holomorphic family \(\pi:X\to S\) and a holomorphic map \(f:T\to S\), the induced family is \(X\times_ST\to T\), with fibre \(X_{f(t)}\) over \(t\). In the moduli category, a morphism over \(f\) includes an isomorphism from the source family to this pullback; these isomorphisms carry the automorphism data.""",r"""Analytic spaces, rather than only manifolds, are allowed so that the required fibre products exist.""",fate=r"""Expository construction in \citet[\S3]{mumford1965picard}.""",status='the construction is standard.',refs=mr)
i(150,'P000239','definition','Kirillov: representability of the moduli functor',r"""Kirillov reformulates universality by assigning to a base the families over it and comparing this with maps into a moduli object.""",r"""Let \(\mathcal F(S)\) be the set of isomorphism classes of admissible families over \(S\). Pullback makes \(\mathcal F\) contravariant. A fine moduli space is an object \(M\) with a natural isomorphism \(\operatorname{Hom}(-,M)\cong\mathcal F\); the universal family corresponds to \(\mathrm{id}_M\). A representing object is unique up to unique isomorphism by the Yoneda argument.""",r"""Isomorphism classes and naturality are essential. A family category with nontrivial automorphisms contains more information than the set-valued functor.""",fate=r"""This is the standard representability reformulation underlying \citet[\S3]{mumford1965picard}, not a new conjecture of Kirillov.""",status='the formulation and uniqueness statement are established.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',refs=mr,speaker='A. A. Kirillov')
i(150,'P000239-P000240','remark','Fine universality versus weaker parameter families',r"""The definitions oscillate between an inducing map existing for every family and being unique; a weaker moduli space is retained when universality fails.""",r"""Fine representability requires a unique classifying map for every family and compatibility with pullback. Existence without uniqueness is a weaker parameter property; local existence near each base point is a versality property and is weaker again. A coarse moduli space has its own universal property for maps out of the moduli problem, and is not defined merely by dropping uniqueness from a universal-family assertion.""",r"""The three notions are separated rather than identifying all weakened formulations with coarse moduli.""",fate=r"""\citet[\S\S3--4]{mumford1965picard} supplies the relevant categories of modular families.""",status='these distinctions are standard and essential for the elliptic example.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',refs=mr)
i(150,'P000240','claim','Nonexistence of a fine moduli space for all pointed elliptic curves',r"""Automorphisms yield a nontrivial family with all fibres isomorphic. Its map to a one-class-per-point space would be constant, forcing a trivial family.""",r"""The functor of isomorphism classes of all pointed complex elliptic families is not represented by the coarse \(j\)-line. For a fixed elliptic curve \(E\), the family over \(\mathbb C^*\) obtained by descending \(\mathbb C\times E\) under \((t,x)\mapsto(t+2\pi i,-x)\) has constant \(j\) but monodromy \(-I\) on \(H_1(E,\mathbb Z)\). It is not the product family, while pullback along a constant map from a fine one-point-per-class parameter space would be a product.""",r"""This explicit isotrivial twist makes the notebook's obstruction precise; the zero section descends.""",fate=r"""This is the obstruction discussed in \citet[\S\S3--4, especially pp.~58--60]{mumford1965picard}.""",status='the nonrepresentability obstruction is established; the moduli stack retains the missing automorphisms.',problem='GP-1968-01',refs=mr)
i(150,'P000240','remark','Rigidification by a homology basis, not merely polarization',r"""The notes say a universal family exists for ``polarized'' curves, explained parenthetically as a chosen homology basis.""",r"""For pointed complex elliptic curves with an oriented integral basis of \(H_1\), the upper half-plane is a fine analytic parameter space and the family \((\mathbb H\times\mathbb C)/\mathbb Z^2\) is universal. This is a marking or rigidification. A principal polarization alone does not eliminate the automorphism \([-1]\); for an unpointed genus-one curve, a homology marking alone also leaves translations.""",r"""The parenthetical basis is taken as the intended data, while the terminology ``polarized'' is corrected. Both the point and the basis are required for the stated rigidification.""",fate=r"""The analytic marked family is used in \citet[\S7]{mumford1965picard}.""",status='the marked fine-moduli construction is established; the polarization-only claim would be false.',literal='false as written',refs=mr)
i(150,'P000240','construction','The moduli object represented by a topology of families',r"""A terminal object is formally adjoined when none exists; its sheaves and cohomology are then studied.""",r"""The family category must retain morphisms together with fibrewise isomorphisms. Given representable isomorphism functors and an appropriate covering rule, one can study its associated site or stack and define sheaves and cohomology there. This replaces the absent universal family over an ordinary coarse parameter space, rather than asserting that formal adjunction alone creates all necessary products and descent.""",r"""The modern stack terminology is explanatory reconstruction; the notebook and Mumford use a topology with a formal final object.""",fate=r"""The precise construction is \citet[\S3]{mumford1965picard}, expanded in records 151--154.""",status='the method is established.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',refs=mr)
i(150,'P000240','remark','Automorphism groups of pointed elliptic curves',r"""Most curves have ``one automorphism'', \(x\mapsto-x\), and some have extra rotations.""",r"""For a pointed complex elliptic curve, the generic origin-preserving automorphism group is cyclic of order two, consisting of the identity and \([-1]\). The square lattice has a group of order four, and the hexagonal lattice a group of order six. Thus ``one'' must mean one nonidentity automorphism in the generic case.""",r"""The origin-preserving condition is retained; without a marked point, all translations are automorphisms.""",fate=r"""\citet[\S4, pp.~57--59]{mumford1965picard}.""",status='the automorphism calculation is established.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',refs=mr)
p('GP-1968-01','A universal family for the elliptic moduli problem',r"""Does an ordinary complex analytic parameter space represent all pointed elliptic families up to isomorphism, and what construction retains a universal family when this fails?""",['1968-150.8'],r'November 2026: the unrigidified family functor is not represented by the coarse j-line. A full homology marking gives the upper-half-plane universal family; the quotient stack or Mumford moduli site retains automorphisms. These facts were already established before the seminar.',mr)
c('P000238','fractional-linear action','(a z+b)/(c z+b)','(a z+b)/(c z+d)','P000238 scan appears to repeat b; P000250–P000251 and the determinant-one matrix fix d.','both','faithful_error')
c('P000240','rigidification terminology','polarized (with a chosen basis of homology)','marked by an oriented integral homology basis, with a distinguished point in genus one','The parenthetical data describe a marking; principal polarization retains the involution.')
c('P000240','generic automorphisms','Most have one automorphism x maps to -x','Most pointed complex elliptic curves have one nonidentity automorphism, hence a group of order2.','Scan confirms one; the identity is also an automorphism.')
save()

# 151.
t(151,'1968-09-09','A. Yu. Geronimus','P000240-P000246','Sites of group actions and families',r"""P000241--P000244 repeat the site axioms and construct the \(\Gamma\)-set example, identify its sheaves with modules, compare an analytic local-isomorphism topology with ordinary topology, and construct products in a moduli category using isomorphism spaces. The principal corrections are the target of a base-changed cover, products versus direct sums over infinitely many orbits, and the finite-group hypothesis in the character formula.""")
i(151,'P000241','definition','Pretopology axioms and base change revisited',r"""The fibre product is a categorical product over a base; a covering \(X_i\to X\) pulled back along \(Y\to X\) is written as a covering of \(X\).""",r"""The base-changed covering is \(\{X_i\times_XY\to Y\}\), not a family with target \(X\). Identity, composite-cover and pullback-cover axioms define the pretopology. A functor between the displayed site categories must preserve the specified finite limits and covers for the intended induced sheaf construction; arrow conventions for geometric morphisms must then be stated separately.""",r"""The displayed target is emended using the universal property and the same axiom as correctly repeated on P000245.""",fate=r"""\citet[pp.~38,41]{mumford1965picard}; compare 1967-143.1--1967-143.2.""",status='the axioms are standard, with the corrected base-change target.',literal='false as written',refs=mr)
i(151,'P000241','construction','The site of Gamma-sets',r"""Objects are \(\Gamma\)-sets, pullbacks are pairs with equal images, covers are jointly surjective, and the terminal object is a point.""",r"""For a discrete group \(\Gamma\), let \(\mathcal T_\Gamma\) be the category of left \(\Gamma\)-sets and equivariant maps. A family covers if its images jointly equal the target. The fibre product has the diagonal action, and the one-point set with trivial action is terminal. This is a site even when \(\Gamma\) is infinite.""",fate=r"""Exact example in \citet[pp.~39--41]{mumford1965picard}.""",status='the construction is established.',refs=mr)
i(151,'P000241','theorem','Sheaves on the group site are modules',r"""A sheaf is determined by \(A=F(\Gamma)\), with \(F(\Gamma/H)=A^H\).""",r"""The category of abelian sheaves on \(\mathcal T_\Gamma\) is equivalent to the category of \(\mathbb Z[\Gamma]\)-modules. A module \(A\) gives \(F_A(S)=\operatorname{Map}_\Gamma(S,A)\); in particular \(F_A(\Gamma/H)=A^H\). The action on \(F(\Gamma)\) comes from equivariant right translations and contravariance, with a consistent left-action convention.""",r"""The acting subgroup in the invariant formula is \(H\); the adjacent unclear letter does not change the displayed equation.""",fate=r"""\citet[pp.~40--41]{mumford1965picard}.""",status='the equivalence and orbit formula are established.',refs=mr)
i(151,'P000242','claim','The value on a disjoint union is a product',r"""For a disjoint union of orbits the notes say to take a direct sum.""",r"""For any disjoint union \(S=\coprod_{\alpha}S_\alpha\), the sheaf condition gives \(F_A(S)=\prod_\alpha F_A(S_\alpha)\). A direct sum agrees only for a finite family of abelian groups. In particular the constant coefficient module can yield arbitrarily supported sections on an infinite disjoint union.""",r"""The scan confirms the Russian words for direct sum; the mathematical correction is product.""",fate=r"""The product, explicitly printed in \citet[p.~40]{mumford1965picard}, also follows immediately from \(\operatorname{Map}_\Gamma\).""",status='the infinite-union direct-sum statement is false.',literal='false as written',refs=mr)
i(151,'P000242','theorem','Cohomology of the terminal group-site object',r"""\(H^q(\cdot,F)=H^q(\Gamma,A)\).""",r"""Under the sheaf-module equivalence, global sections are \(A^\Gamma\). Their right derived functors are group cohomology: \(H^q(\mathcal T_\Gamma,F_A)\cong H^q(\Gamma,A)\) for every \(q\ge0\). The terminal object's underlying set being a point does not make these groups vanish.""",fate=r"""\citet[p.~41]{mumford1965picard}.""",status='the comparison is established.',refs=mr)
i(151,'P000242','claim','H2 with integer coefficients and one-dimensional characters',r"""\(H^2(\Gamma,\mathbb Z)\) is identified with all one-dimensional complex representations of \(\Gamma\), without a group restriction.""",r"""If \(\Gamma\) is finite, the exact sequence \(0\to\mathbb Z\to\mathbb C\xrightarrow{\exp(2\pi i\,\cdot)}\mathbb C^*\to1\), with trivial actions, gives \(H^2(\Gamma,\mathbb Z)\cong H^1(\Gamma,\mathbb C^*)=\operatorname{Hom}(\Gamma,\mathbb C^*)\), since positive-degree cohomology with coefficients in \(\mathbb C\) vanishes by averaging. More generally the same conclusion needs the relevant vanishing of \(H^1\) and \(H^2\) with \(\mathbb C\) coefficients.""",r"""The claim fails for \(\Gamma=\mathbb Z\): \(H^2=0\), whereas its complex characters form \(\mathbb C^*\).""",fate=r"""This is a direct application of the derived-functor formalism recalled in \citet[p.~41]{mumford1965picard}; the additional finite-group hypothesis is not visible in this September note.""",status='the corrected finite-group formula is established; the unrestricted one is false.',literal='false as written',match='partial',refs=mr)
i(151,'P000242','construction','The analytic local-isomorphism topology',r"""Analytic manifolds and analytic maps are used, with jointly surjective unramified local coverings; fibre products are declared to exist.""",r"""For a fixed complex analytic space \(X\), the analytic \(\acute{e}\)tale site has local biholomorphisms \(U\to X\) as objects and jointly surjective families as covers. Its fibre products exist. If one instead uses all holomorphic maps among all analytic manifolds, arbitrary fibre products need not remain manifolds; the larger category of analytic spaces repairs this, as the next lecture explicitly acknowledges.""",r"""Discreteness of fibres alone does not mean unramified: \(z\mapsto z^2\) has discrete fibres but ramifies at zero.""",fate=r"""\citet[\S2, pp.~44--45]{mumford1965picard}; the internal correction is occurrence 1968-152.1.""",status='the analytic-space site is established; the blanket manifold pullback assertion is false.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',refs=mr)
i(151,'P000242','theorem','Comparison with ordinary analytic sheaf cohomology',r"""The topology's cohomology on \(X\) equals the cohomology of the restricted sheaf on the usual topology because coverings split locally.""",r"""The inclusion of ordinary open subsets of a complex analytic space \(X\) into its local-biholomorphism site induces an equivalence of sheaf categories. For an abelian sheaf \(F\), \(H^q(X_{\mathrm{an},\acute{e}t},F)\cong H^q(X,F|_{\mathrm{opens}})\). Locally each local biholomorphism is an isomorphism onto an ordinary open subset, providing the comparison basis.""",r"""This is an analytic local-isomorphism comparison; it is not an assertion that algebraic \(\acute{e}\)tale cohomology with arbitrary coefficients equals ordinary topology over every field.""",fate=r"""\citet[pp.~44--45]{mumford1965picard}.""",status='the comparison is established.',refs=mr)
i(151,'P000243','construction','The site of families and cohomology of an individual family',r"""Objects are analytic families \(\pi:X\to S\), morphisms induce pullbacks, and covers are covers of bases. The cohomology of \(\pi\) is that of \(S\).""",r"""For a fixed family \(\pi\), the local-isomorphism site of its pullbacks \(X\times_SU\to U\) is equivalent to the local-isomorphism site of \(S\). Thus a sheaf restricted to this slice has the corresponding cohomology \(H^q(S,F_\pi)\). Morphisms of families include a Cartesian square and its chosen fibrewise isomorphism.""",r"""The identification is for the slice over the particular family. It does not identify the cohomology of the entire moduli problem with the cohomology of one arbitrary base.""",fate=r"""The family-site construction is \citet[\S3]{mumford1965picard}; compare its induced-topology definition on p.~42.""",status='the slice comparison is established.',refs=mr)
i(151,'P000243-P000244','construction','Products over the formal moduli object are isomorphism spaces',r"""The direct product of two families cannot simply have base \(S_1\times S_2\); the notes later count automorphisms as multiplicities. An earlier sentence says a curve's product with itself is itself.""",r"""For proper smooth pointed genus-one families, or smooth proper curves of genus at least two, the product over the moduli object has base \(I=\operatorname{Isom}_{S_1\times S_2}(\operatorname{pr}_1^*X_1,\operatorname{pr}_2^*X_2)\). A point consists of \((s_1,s_2,\alpha)\) with \(\alpha:X_{1,s_1}\cong X_{2,s_2}\). Its fibre over \((s_1,s_2)\) is empty if the curves are nonisomorphic and otherwise a torsor under their automorphism group. A self-product therefore has one point for each automorphism, not necessarily one point.""",r"""The Isom space is an analytic space; smoothness or local biholomorphy requires additional conditions on the families. The lower-page multiplicity remark corrects the preceding set-level simplification.""",fate=r"""Exact construction in \citet[\S3, pp.~49--53]{mumford1965picard}.""",status='the Isom construction is established and retains the automorphism information lost by a coarse quotient.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',refs=mr)
i(151,'P000244','definition','Coverings of the formal terminal object',r"""A family \(\{\pi_i\to M\}\) covers if for every \(\pi\), the products \(\pi_i\times_M\pi\to\pi\) form a genuine covering. All families together need not be a covering.""",r"""An atlas family covers the moduli object when every base change by an admissible family is represented by a jointly surjective family of local isomorphisms of bases. This requires both local deformation completeness and the appropriate unramified condition. Merely listing a representative of every curve, or including every possible family, does not ensure that each component map is eligible as an \(\acute{e}\)tale cover.""",r"""The covering class is fixed before applying joint surjectivity; adding non-\(\acute{e}\)tale families is not automatically permitted.""",fate=r"""\citet[\S3, pp.~50--54]{mumford1965picard}.""",status='the base-change test is established.',refs=mr)
c('P000241','base change of a cover','X_i times_X Y maps to X, cover','X_i times_X Y maps to Y, cover','Scan visibly has X; the defining pullback axiom and P000245 have targetY.')
c('P000242','union of group orbits','direct sum','direct product for an arbitrary possibly infinite disjoint union','Scan confirms direct sum; Mumford1965 p.40 prints a product, as required by the sheaf equalizer.')
c('P000242','group cohomology characters','H2(Gamma,Z)=all complex one-dimensional representations','Valid for finite Gamma (or appropriate H1/H2(Gamma,C) vanishing); not for an arbitrary group.','Gamma=Z has H2=0 and Hom(Gamma,C*)=C*.')
c('P000244','self-product and multiplicities','If X1=X2, the product is itself','The product over the moduli object includes every isomorphism; its base is Isom(X1,X2).','The same page subsequently records automorphism multiplicities; Mumford1965 §3 gives the representing Isom space.')
save()

# 152.
t(152,'1968-09-16','A. Yu. Geronimus','P000244-P000249','Analytic spaces, cohomology comparison and moduli atlases',r"""On P000245--P000247 Geronimus explicitly repairs the previous lecture by replacing analytic manifolds by analytic spaces. He repeats the site and sheaf axioms, recalls sheafification and derived cohomology, gives a local-acyclicity comparison criterion, and formulates covering and local-extension conditions for a moduli atlas. These are expository parts of the Mumford programme, not new general existence theorems for arbitrary moduli problems.""")
i(152,'P000245','remark','Analytic spaces are required for general fibre products',r"""The opening apology says that everywhere one should read analytic spaces, not analytic manifolds, because fibre products must exist.""",r"""The category of complex analytic spaces admits fibre products of holomorphic maps. The subcategory of manifolds does not: the pullback of \(z\mapsto z^2\) along the map of a point to zero is the nonreduced analytic space with local ring \(\mathbb C\{z\}/(z^2)\). Even among reduced spaces, the equation \(x^2=y^2\) gives a singular pullback.""",r"""Nonreduced structure is retained in the first example; passing only to point sets would erase the obstruction. This is the lecturer's own correction, not a modern conjectural emendation.""",fate=r"""The distinction is already built into \citet[\S\S2--3]{mumford1965picard}. It corrects occurrence 1968-151.7.""",status='the category correction is established and necessary.',refs=mr)
i(152,'P000245','definition','Fibre products and the three covering axioms repeated',r"""The universal square and its unique factorization are redrawn, followed by composition, identity and pullback of covers.""",r"""For maps \(X\to Z\leftarrow Y\), \(X\times_ZY\) represents compatible pairs of maps into \(X,Y\). A pretopology has identity covers, composites of covering families as covers, and base-changed covers \(X_i\times_XY\to Y\). These statements include the existence of the indicated pullbacks.""",r"""The undeciphered letter above the self-map \(X\to X\) is understood as the identity map, as required by the axiom; no further mathematical content is assigned to the cancelled word.""",fate=r"""Already given in \citet[p.~38]{mumford1965picard}; this is a recurrence of 1967-143.1--1967-143.2 and 1968-151.1.""",status='the formulation is standard.',refs=mr)
i(152,'P000245','definition','Abelian presheaves and effective gluing',r"""A presheaf is contravariant, and a sheaf has a unique global section inducing every compatible family of local sections.""",r"""An abelian presheaf is a functor \(F:\mathcal C^{\mathrm{op}}\to\mathrm{Ab}\). For each cover \(\{U_i\to U\}\), the map \(F(U)\to\prod_iF(U_i)\) identifies \(F(U)\) with the equalizer of the two restrictions to \(\prod_{i,j}F(U_i\times_UU_j)\). Thus compatibility is equality after the two projections, and both existence and uniqueness are required.""",fate=r"""\citet[p.~39]{mumford1965picard}; a repeated definition rather than an additional theorem.""",status='the definition is established.',refs=mr)
i(152,'P000246','construction','Canonical sheafification',r"""A parenthesis asserts a canonical procedure turning every presheaf into a sheaf for any topology.""",r"""For a site, the inclusion of abelian sheaves into abelian presheaves has a left adjoint \(F\mapsto aF\), characterized by \(\operatorname{Hom}(aF,G)\cong\operatorname{Hom}(F,G)\) for sheaves \(G\). For abelian groups sheafification is exact. In particular a presheaf may be nonzero while its associated sheaf vanishes because its sections vanish locally.""",r"""The universal property and exactness specify the construction used in the following comparison criterion; no claim that sections are unchanged is made.""",fate=r"""This is standard machinery in \citet[\S1]{mumford1965picard} and the sheaf-theoretic framework of \citet[\S3]{grothendieck1957homological}.""",status='the construction is established.',refs=mr+['grothendieck1957homological'])
i(152,'P000246','theorem','Derived cohomology, exact sequences and injective acyclicity',r"""The notes prescribe \(H^0(U,F)=F(U)\), a long exact sequence for short exact sequences, enough injectives, and zero cohomology for injective sheaves.""",r"""The category of abelian sheaves on a site has enough injectives. Deriving the left-exact sections functor gives \(H^q(U,F)\), with \(H^0(U,F)=F(U)\), functorial connecting morphisms, and the long exact cohomology sequence. An injective sheaf \(I\) has \(H^q(U,I)=0\) for \(q>0\); its degree-zero sections need not vanish.""",r"""The omitted positive-degree qualification is restored. Cohomology on \(U\) means cohomology in the slice site.""",fate=r"""\citet[pp.~37--39]{mumford1965picard}; \citet[\S\S2--3]{grothendieck1957homological}.""",status='the corrected derived-functor assertions are established.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',refs=mr+['grothendieck1957homological'])
i(152,'P000246','method','Local-acyclicity criterion for comparison of sites',r"""For a structure-compatible functor \(f:T_1\to T_2\), the notation \(f^*F(U)=F(f(U))\) is used. If the sheaf associated with \(U\mapsto H^i(f(U),F)\) is zero, the displayed cohomology map is an isomorphism.""",r"""Let a functor \(u:\mathcal C\to\mathcal D\) induce a geometric morphism \(f:\operatorname{Sh}(\mathcal D)\to\operatorname{Sh}(\mathcal C)\) whose direct image is \(f_*F(U)=F(uU)\), and suppose the global-sections functors compose in the usual way. If \(R^if_*F=0\) for every \(i>0\), the Leray spectral sequence gives \(H^q(\mathcal C,f_*F)\cong H^q(\mathcal D,F)\). The higher direct image is computed by sheafifying the presheaf of cohomology over the inverse-image slices; when these are represented by \(uU\), this is \(U\mapsto H^i(\mathcal D/uU,F)\). The same criterion applies to the corresponding slices over an object.""",r"""The notebook's star labels precomposition, which is the direct-image functor for the geometric morphism in this convention. The positive-degree condition and the precise slice interpretation are supplied; a condition at \(i=0\) would generally force the coefficient sheaf itself to vanish.""",fate=r"""The exact application appears in \citet[\S7, p.~80, vanishing of \(R^iy_*\) for \(i>0\)]{mumford1965picard}; the homological argument is the Leray formalism of \citet[\S3]{grothendieck1957homological}.""",status='the criterion is established under the indicated functorial and acyclicity hypotheses.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',refs=mr+['grothendieck1957homological'])
i(152,'P000246','construction','A terminal moduli object and its candidate atlas',r"""A new category \(T_M\) adjoins a terminal object. Candidate covering families must contain every isomorphism type, with each type occurring only discretely.""",r"""For a moduli category with representable isomorphism functors, an atlas is a family of objects whose maps to the moduli object are locally isomorphisms in the chosen topology and are jointly surjective after every base change. Meeting every isomorphism class is the pointwise-surjectivity part; discrete occurrence is a necessary unramified-type condition in this setting. Those two conditions alone do not prove local universality or the existence of the required pullbacks.""",r"""The local extension requirement on the next page supplies information beyond the two enumerated conditions. Formal adjunction of a terminal object is not treated as an existence proof for a fine moduli space.""",fate=r"""The precise modular-family construction is \citet[\S3, pp.~49--54]{mumford1965picard}; compare 1968-151.10--1968-151.11.""",status='the atlas construction is established for the stated moduli problem, with the local hypotheses retained.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',refs=mr)
i(152,'P000247','method','Local extension property of a modular family',r"""A morphism from a subfamily over a submanifold is required to extend locally to a neighbourhood in the base of an arbitrary family; the picture calls it a tubular neighbourhood.""",r"""A sufficient precise version is the following. Let \(X\to S\) be a modular analytic family such that, for every test family \(Y\to T\), its isomorphism space \(I=\operatorname{Isom}(Y,X)\to T\) is a local biholomorphism. A family morphism defined on an analytic subspace \(T_0\subset T\) gives a section of \(I\to T\) over \(T_0\). Near each point of \(T_0\), the inverse of the appropriate local biholomorphic branch extends that section, hence extends the family morphism.""",r"""This is a local assertion, not a global extension theorem or existence of a holomorphic tubular neighbourhood for every analytic subspace. The displayed crossed arrow does not uniquely encode a stronger formal deformation criterion.""",fate=r"""The isomorphism-space criterion and modular families are treated in \citet[\S3, pp.~50--54]{mumford1965picard}. The notebook gives an informal extension formulation of this mechanism.""",status='the precise local lifting statement is established; the broad tubular-neighbourhood wording needs the reconstruction above.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',refs=mr,confidence='medium')
c('P000245','opening category correction','Earlier lectures: analytic manifolds','Analytic spaces, including singular or nonreduced fibre products where required.','Geronimus explicitly apologizes and makes this correction in the notebook.','both','provenance')
c('P000246','injective acyclicity','For an injective sheaf cohomology is zero','Positive-degree cohomology is zero; H0 is the sections group.','The same page states H0(U,F)=F(U); injectivity does not force sections to vanish.')
c('P000246','comparison criterion degree','Associated sheaf of U to Hi(f(U),F) is zero','Require vanishing for every i>0; identify the higher direct-image sheaves and fix the direction convention.','Mumford1965 p.80 states R^iy_*=0 for i>0. The source does not specify the range of i.')
c('P000247','extension neighbourhood','Extension to a tubular neighbourhood','Local extension of the family morphism near each point, under the modular local-isomorphism hypothesis.','The sketch does not establish a global holomorphic tubular neighbourhood; Mumford1965 §3 gives the Isom-space formulation.','both','ambiguity')
save()

# 153.
t(153,'1968-09-16','V. P. Palamodov','P000247-P000251','Jet bundles and the Spencer resolution',r"""The mathematical text is P000248--P000249. Palamodov introduces the jet prolongation of smooth functions, the holonomicity equations and the finite Spencer resolution. The notes expressly direct readers to Sweeney's Acta Mathematica paper, which was printed in June 1968 after a September 1966 submission. The listed researchers include Sweeney, Goldschmidt, Guillemin, Quillen, Sternberg and Singer; the unclear name is not treated as a separate attribution. The construction for unrestricted jets is separated from exactness and solvability for a general differential equation.""")
sr=['sweeney1968neumann','spencer1969overdetermined']
i(153,'P000248','method','Resolving compatibility conditions for a differential system',r"""The Spencer resolution is presented as a method for solving \(p(x,D)u=w\), with a matrix \(p\) and vector-valued unknown and data.""",r"""A differential operator \(P:\mathcal E\to\mathcal F\) of order \(\mu\) factors as \(\mathcal E\xrightarrow{j_\mu}J_\mu E\xrightarrow{p_\mu}F\). Prolonged equations and their compatibility operators yield Spencer complexes under constant-rank and regularity hypotheses. Their formal exactness is distinct from exactness on smooth sections. A locally exact compatibility complex gives local solvability for data satisfying its next compatibility equation; the unrestricted-jet resolution alone does not prove this for every \(P\).""",r"""The hypotheses are restored at the level of the method. Neither formal integrability nor the use of jets is presented as an unconditional smooth existence theorem.""",fate=r"""The expressly named source is \citet[Introduction; Chapter~I, \S3 and Proposition~8.3]{sweeney1968neumann}, received 4 September 1966, revised 30 November 1967, and printed June 1968. It gives exactness results for regular constant-coefficient systems and real-analytic germs under its stated hypotheses. Compare \citet[\S\S1.2,1.6]{spencer1969overdetermined}.""",status='the method and its specified applications are established; no general smooth-solvability claim follows from the note.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',refs=sr)
i(153,'P000248','construction','Finite jet bundles and an injective map of sheaves',r"""For the sheaf \(\mathcal O\) of smooth-function germs, \(j_\mu:\mathcal O\to J_\mu\) is said to be an embedding. The zeroth-order case is followed by an ambiguous zero symbol.""",r"""On a smooth \(n\)-manifold, \(J_\mu\) is the vector bundle of \(\mu\)-jets of scalar functions. A local section of this bundle has coordinates \(\phi_I(x)\), \(0\le |I|\le\mu\), using unnormalized derivatives. Prolongation sends \(f\) to the section \(j_\mu f=(\partial^If)_I\). As a map of sheaves of real vector spaces it is injective because its zeroth component is \(f\). It is not the evaluation map from a function germ to its finite jet at one point, which is not injective. For \(\mu=0\), \(J_0\cong\mathcal O\) and \(j_0\) is the identity.""",r"""Bundles, sheaves of their sections, and individual jet fibres are distinguished. The subsequent map at order zero is to zero, yielding \(0\to\mathcal O\xrightarrow{\mathrm{id}}\mathcal O\to0\).""",fate=r"""\citet[Chapter~I, \S1 and Proposition~6.2]{sweeney1968neumann}; \citet[\S\S1.1,1.3]{spencer1969overdetermined}.""",status='the construction and sheaf-level injectivity are established.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',refs=sr)
i(153,'P000248-P000249','theorem','Holonomic jet sections are characterized by derivative compatibility',r"""The notes first compare \(\partial\phi_0/\partial x_j\) with \(\phi_j\), then require \(\partial_j\phi_I=\phi_{I+1_j}\) for every \(|I|\le\mu-1\).""",r"""A smooth local jet section \(\phi=(\phi_I)_{|I|\le\mu}\) is holonomic, meaning \(\phi=j_\mu f\) for a smooth function \(f\), if and only if \(\partial_j\phi_I=\phi_{I+e_j}\) for all \(j\) and \(|I|\le\mu-1\). Necessity follows by differentiation; sufficiency follows inductively from \(f=\phi_0\). For normalized Taylor coefficients the formula would instead contain the corresponding multi-index factors.""",r"""The derivative rather than Taylor-coefficient normalization is fixed by the first-order example. Gelfand's intervention is the coordinate presentation, not a separate conjecture.""",fate=r"""This is the kernel statement in \citet[Chapter~I, Proposition~5.1(ii)]{sweeney1968neumann} and \citet[equation~(1.3.2)]{spencer1969overdetermined}.""",status='the criterion is established and directly verified by induction.',refs=sr)
i(153,'P000249','construction','The Spencer operator as actual minus formal differentiation',r"""The first operator is \(\sum_j(\partial_j\phi_I-\phi_{I+e_j})dx_j\), and its continuation is called \(D=d-\delta\).""",r"""For a jet-valued differential form \(\phi\in\Omega^r\otimes J_k\), the Spencer operator is locally \((D\phi)_I=\sum_j dx_j\wedge(\partial_j\phi_I-\phi_{I+e_j})\), \(|I|\le k-1\). Thus \(D:\Omega^r\otimes J_k\to\Omega^{r+1}\otimes J_{k-1}\). It is a globally defined first-order differential operator; in these coordinates it is the difference of actual and formal exterior differentiation. The two summands separately depend on coordinates, whereas their difference does not.""",r"""The formula includes exterior degree, jet order, and the order of wedge factors. It is not linear over smooth functions for the usual module structures.""",fate=r"""\citet[Chapter~I, \S5, equation~(5.4)]{sweeney1968neumann}; \citet[equation~(1.3.1)\(^{\prime}\)]{spencer1969overdetermined}.""",status='the operator is established; its invariant character is essential to the resolution.',refs=sr)
i(153,'P000249','claim','The one-form formula requires a wedge-order correction',r"""The displayed continuation is \(d(\phi_I^jdx_j)-\phi_{I+e_s}^jdx_j\wedge dx_s\), with the derivative-index correction also unclear.""",r"""With \(D=d-\delta\) as in the preceding degree-zero formula, the correct expression is 
\[
D(\phi_I^jdx_j)=\sum_{s,j}(\partial_s\phi_I^j-\phi_{I+e_s}^j)\,dx_s\wedge dx_j.
\]
Equivalently the formal term is \(+\sum_{s,j}\phi_{I+e_s}^jdx_j\wedge dx_s\). Retaining the printed minus sign together with the reversed wedge order is inconsistent with the previous definition and does not generally give \(D^2=0\).""",r"""The scan confirms the displayed order \(dx_j\wedge dx_s\); the choice \(e_s\) is dictated by the differentiation direction. The repair is mathematical, not a claim that the transcription omitted a legible sign.""",fate=r"""The corrected order is explicit in \citet[equation~(1.3.1)\(^{\prime}\)]{spencer1969overdetermined}; compare \citet[Chapter~I, equation~(5.4)]{sweeney1968neumann}.""",status='the literal sign-and-order combination is false; the corrected formula gives the standard differential.',literal='false as written',refs=sr)
i(153,'P000249','theorem','The finite exact Spencer sequence for unrestricted jets',r"""The sequence \(\mathcal O\to J_\mu\to T^*\otimes J_{\mu-1}\to\Lambda^2T^*\otimes J_{\mu-2}\to\cdots\) is said to terminate and be exact.""",r"""For every \(\mu\ge0\), the sequence of sheaves of real vector spaces 
\[
0\longrightarrow\mathcal C^\infty_M\xrightarrow{j_\mu}J_\mu\xrightarrow D\Omega^1\otimes J_{\mu-1}\xrightarrow D\cdots\xrightarrow D\Omega^n\otimes J_{\mu-n}\longrightarrow0
\]
is exact, with \(J_k=0\) for \(k<0\). The identities \(d^2=\delta^2=0\) and \(d\delta+\delta d=0\) give \(D^2=0\); exactness follows inductively in jet order using the exact polynomial Koszul complex for the highest-order symbols. The same result holds for the jet bundle of any smooth vector bundle.""",r"""This resolves the sheaf of unrestricted smooth sections. For a subbundle of jets defined by a differential equation, exactness is a separate analytic issue, as explained in 1968-153.1. The individual maps are not morphisms of the usual smooth-function module structures.""",fate=r"""Exact publication match: \citet[Chapter~I, Proposition~6.2, p.~238]{sweeney1968neumann}; reproduced as equation~(1.3.3) with proof in \citet[\S1.3]{spencer1969overdetermined}.""",status='the finite jet resolution is established and was available before the seminar.',refs=sr)
i(153,'P000248','remark','The unresolved comparison with a higher-order pseudo-object',r"""A marginal comparison reads either \ru{псевдогр.} or \ru{псевдооп.}, followed by ``one order higher''.""",r"""No mathematical assertion beyond the legible jet construction can be uniquely recovered from this phrase. The first reading might refer to pseudogroups and the second to an operator construction, but the note does not supply a definition or a formula deciding between them.""",r"""Neither reading is silently promoted to an additional theorem; the names listed above it belong to the surrounding Spencer-theory attribution.""",cls='F',fate=r"""\citet[Introduction and bibliography]{sweeney1968neumann} explicitly connects Spencer theory with pseudogroups, making that reading plausible but not conclusive. The alternative remains a transcription ambiguity.""",status='the marginal comparison remains unidentified; it is not an open mathematical problem.',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',refs=['sweeney1968neumann'],confidence='low')
c('P000248','jet embedding','j_mu is an embedding','It embeds the sheaf of smooth functions into the sheaf of jet sections; finite-jet evaluation at a point is not injective.','The zeroth component of the prolonged section is the function itself; Sweeney1968 ChapterI §1.')
c('P000249','Spencer one-form sign','d(phi_I^j dx_j)-phi_(I+e_s)^j dx_j wedge dx_s','d(phi_I^j dx_j)-phi_(I+e_s)^j dx_s wedge dx_j','Scan confirms minus with reversed wedge order; Spencer1969 equation(1.3.1)prime and the requirement D squared=0 fix the order.')
save()

# 154.
t(154,'1968-09-23','A. Yu. Geronimus; I. R. Shafarevich','P000249-P000254','The elliptic moduli site and a foliation example',r"""P000250--P000251 applies Mumford's machinery to pointed elliptic curves: the upper-half-plane family, the modular action, level-three comparison and the class of order twelve. The integral cohomology formula needs its degree-zero and periodic-even-degree corrections, and the assertion over general fields concerns the Picard invariant rather than ordinary integral Betti cohomology. On P000252 Shafarevich proposes a site of transverse patches for a differential equation and makes a compressed Euler-characteristic remark. The following Kazhdan and Palamodov headings on P000253--P000254 belong to records 155--156 and are excluded here.""")
er=mr+['hain2008moduli']
i(154,'P000250','definition','Pointed elliptic families and Cartesian morphisms',r"""The objects are analytic families of complex tori with a distinguished section \(\sigma:S\to X\).""",r"""An analytic family of pointed elliptic curves is a proper smooth holomorphic map \(\pi:E\to S\) with connected genus-one fibres, together with a holomorphic section \(e:S\to E\). Morphisms consist of a map of bases and a section-preserving isomorphism with the pulled-back family. The section makes each fibre an elliptic group curve and removes translation automorphisms, but retains the origin-preserving finite automorphisms.""",r"""Properness, smoothness and connected fibres specify the intended family category; arbitrary maps with pointwise torus fibres are not silently admitted.""",fate=r"""The category is the elliptic-curve case of \citet[\S4]{mumford1965picard}; see also \citet[\S2]{hain2008moduli}.""",status='the definition is established.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',refs=er)
i(154,'P000250','claim','The coarse j-line has no smooth family realizing its identity parameter',r"""The quotient \(S'=\mathbb H/\Gamma\) is called coarse; the notes say there is a family with base \(S'\), then explain that the family over \(\mathbb H\) is not induced from it and that isotrivial twists are missed.""",r"""For \(\Gamma=SL_2(\mathbb Z)\), the coarse quotient is the \(j\)-line. There is no smooth pointed elliptic family over the whole coarse line whose \(j\)-invariant is the identity. Locally near the square or hexagonal elliptic point, a family over a disc has a lifted period map to \(\mathbb H\); the coarse map is locally a square or cube of a local coordinate, so it cannot compose to a simple parameter on that disc. Separately, nontrivial isotrivial twists show that a pointwise classifying coarse map cannot classify all families.""",r"""The statement ``there is a family'' is false if it means one realizing each coarse parameter, as the surrounding discussion suggests. Trivial families over the line certainly exist but do not have the required classifying map. The two obstructions are distinguished.""",fate=r"""The exceptional local parameters are discussed in \citet[\S4, p.~59]{mumford1965picard}. The local lifting obstruction and isotrivial construction are explicit in \citet[Exercises~25--26, \S3.6]{hain2008moduli}. This repeats GP-1968-01.""",status='the coarse-versus-fine distinction and nonexistence are established.',literal='false as written',problem='GP-1968-01',refs=er)
i(154,'P000250-P000251','construction','The marked universal family and its modular action',r"""The family is \((\mathbb H\times\mathbb C)/((\tau,z)\sim(\tau,z+m\tau+n))\). The group acts by \(\tau'=(a\tau+b)/(c\tau+d)\), \(z'=z/(c\tau+d)\).""",r"""The quotient \(\mathcal E_{\mathbb H}=(\mathbb H\times\mathbb C)/\mathbb Z^2\), with translations \(z\mapsto z+m\tau+n\), is a holomorphic family with zero section and a distinguished oriented homology basis. Every \(\gamma=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{smallmatrix}\right)\in SL_2(\mathbb Z)\) lifts by the displayed formula because \((c\tau+d)^{-1}(\mathbb Z\tau+\mathbb Z)=\mathbb Z\gamma\tau+\mathbb Z\). The quotient family over the analytic stack \([\mathbb H/SL_2(\mathbb Z)]\) is universal in the family-category sense.""",r"""The notebook's underlined ``problem'' is the task of specifying this lifted action, answered on the next page; it is not a newly open problem. Stack notation is modern reconstruction.""",fate=r"""\citet[\S7]{mumford1965picard}; an explicit modern formulation is \citet[\S3.6, Theorem~3.18]{hain2008moduli}.""",status='the construction and action are established.',refs=er)
i(154,'P000250','remark','Three marked boundary points represent two exceptional orbits',r"""The fundamental-domain drawing marks three fixed points and says that branching occurs there.""",r"""For the effective \(PSL_2(\mathbb Z)\)-action on \(\mathbb H\), there are two orbits with nontrivial stabilizers, represented by \(i\) and \(e^{2\pi i/3}\), with orders two and three. The two boundary representatives of the hexagonal orbit account for the third marked point in a closed fundamental domain. For \(SL_2(\mathbb Z)\), \(-I\) fixes every base point, so the generic stabilizer is \(C_2\), and the exceptional stabilizers are \(C_4,C_6\).""",r"""Counting points drawn in one closed domain is separated from counting orbits and from the non-effective central action.""",fate=r"""\citet[\S4]{mumford1965picard}; \citet[\S3.5, Example~3.14]{hain2008moduli}.""",status='the stabilizer and branching descriptions are established after this qualification.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',refs=er)
i(154,'P000251','construction','Constant coefficients and the cohomology of one family',r"""The coefficient sheaf has one copy of \(\mathbb Z\) for each connected component, and \(H^q(\pi,\mathbb Z)=H^q(S,\mathbb Z)\).""",r"""On the local-isomorphism slice over a family \(\pi:E\to S\), the constant coefficient sheaf assigns locally constant integer-valued functions to each base. If a base \(U\) is locally connected, this group is \(\prod_{\pi_0(U)}\mathbb Z\). The slice-site comparison identifies \(H^q(\pi,\mathbb Z)\) with ordinary sheaf cohomology \(H^q(S,\mathbb Z)\). These are not the cohomology groups of the total space \(E\), nor yet those of the terminal moduli object.""",r"""The finite three-summand drawing is harmless; for an infinite set of components one must take a product. Connected components are open for the analytic spaces under discussion.""",fate=r"""The comparison uses \citet[\S\S2--3]{mumford1965picard} and repeats 1968-151.8--1968-151.9 with specified coefficients.""",status='the slice identification is established.',refs=mr)
i(154,'P000251','method','The level-three subsite and comparison with group cohomology',r"""A subcategory of \(\Gamma\)-sets has point stabilizers inside \(\Gamma_3\). The assignment \(S\mapsto(\mathbb H\times S)/\Gamma\) induces a cohomology isomorphism after adjoining terminal objects.""",r"""Let \(\Gamma=SL_2(\mathbb Z)\) and \(\Gamma(3)=\ker(\Gamma\to SL_2(\mathbb Z/3))\). This subgroup is torsion-free. For a discrete \(\Gamma\)-set \(S\) with all stabilizers contained in \(\Gamma(3)\), the diagonal action on \(\mathbb H\times S\) is free and its quotient is a complex analytic base carrying the descended elliptic family. These objects form the comparison subsite. Contractibility of \(\mathbb H\), local acyclicity and the covering criterion give \(H^q(\mathcal M_{1,1}^{\mathrm{an}},\mathbb Z)\cong H^q(\Gamma,\mathbb Z)\).""",r"""The freeness is for the diagonal action on these admissible objects; the full modular action on the upper half-plane is not free. The formal terminal object is adjoined because the one-point \(\Gamma\)-set does not satisfy the stabilizer restriction.""",fate=r"""Exact construction and comparison: \citet[\S7, pp.~79--80]{mumford1965picard}. The classifying-space interpretation is \citet[Proposition~3.13]{hain2008moduli}.""",status='the comparison is established.',refs=er)
i(154,'P000251','claim','Integral cohomology of the elliptic moduli site is periodic',r"""The formula is transcribed as \(H^q(M,\mathbb Z)=0\) for \(q\ne2\), and \(\mathbb Z_{12}\) for \(q=2\); the degree sign is heavily retraced.""",r"""For the uncompactified analytic moduli stack of pointed elliptic curves,
\[
H^q(\mathcal M_{1,1}^{\mathrm{an}},\mathbb Z)=
\begin{cases}\mathbb Z,&q=0,\\\mathbb Z/12,&q>0\text{ even},\\0,&q\text{ odd}.\end{cases}
\]
More precisely its ring is \(\mathbb Z[c]/(12c)\), with \(|c|=2\). This differs both from the coarse \(j\)-line, whose positive-degree integral cohomology vanishes, and from the compactified moduli stack.""",r"""The higher-degree correction follows from \(SL_2(\mathbb Z)\cong C_4*_{C_2}C_6\). In every positive even degree the Mayer--Vietoris map \(\mathbb Z/4\oplus\mathbb Z/6\to\mathbb Z/2\) is \((a,b)\mapsto a-b\) modulo two, is surjective, and has cyclic kernel of order twelve. The odd groups vanish; degree zero is \(\mathbb Z\). Choosing the degree-two class restricting to generators of both cyclic groups gives the displayed ring. This is an explicit verification of a correction, not a novel cohomology theorem.""",fate=r"""The moduli-to-group comparison is \citet[\S7]{mumford1965picard}; the degree-one and degree-two computation and group presentation are in \citet[Proposition~3.13 and presentation~(3)]{hain2008moduli}. The periodic extension is verified by the amalgam calculation above.""",status='the corrected integral cohomology is established; the literal concentration in degree two is false.',literal='false as written',refs=er)
i(154,'P000251','claim','The assertion over other fields concerns the Picard group',r"""The notes say that the answer remains true over any field of characteristic different from two.""",r"""For every field \(k\), the algebraic Picard group of the stack of elliptic curves is \(\operatorname{Pic}(\mathcal M_{1,1,k})\cong\mathbb Z/12\), generated by the Hodge line bundle. This is a statement about invertible sheaves. Ordinary integral Betti cohomology is the complex-analytic invariant of the preceding item and is not defined by that formula for an arbitrary field.""",r"""The text switches invariants without explaining the comparison. Mumford's theorem as published assumes characteristic different from both two and three. Its Picard conclusion over fields of characteristic three, and indeed two, has a later exact extension; this does not extend the entire displayed Betti-cohomology table to all fields.""",cls='B',fate=r"""\citet[\S6, Main Theorem, p.~73]{mumford1965picard} proves the characteristic-not-two-or-three case. \citet[Theorem~1.1]{fultonolsson2010picard}, circulated on 17 April 2007 and published in 2010, proves \(\operatorname{Pic}(\mathcal M_{1,1,S})\cong\mathbb Z/12\oplus\operatorname{Pic}(\mathbb A^1_S)\) for reduced \(S\), or when two is invertible on \(S\). For \(S=\operatorname{Spec}k\), the second summand is zero.""",status='the algebraic Picard assertion is established for every field. The literal general-field cohomology statement is under-specified until its invariant and topology are fixed.',literal='not determinable',match='partial',refs=mr+['fultonolsson2010picard'])
i(154,'P000251','remark','What the invariant modulo twelve means for a family',r"""A boxed conclusion says that families have an invariant modulo twelve.""",r"""The universal Hodge line bundle \(\lambda\) generates the cyclic Picard group of order twelve. For a smooth pointed elliptic family \(\pi:E\to S\), its pullback is \(\lambda_S=\pi_*\Omega^1_{E/S}\), and the nonvanishing discriminant gives a canonical trivialization of \(\lambda_S^{\otimes12}\). In the complex analytic setting, \(c_1(\lambda_S)\in H^2(S,\mathbb Z)\) is the pullback of the universal order-twelve class and is killed by twelve.""",r"""A family over an arbitrary base does not automatically yield a scalar in \(\mathbb Z/12\). It yields a line-bundle class, or its cohomology class on that base, whose universal source has order twelve.""",fate=r"""\citet[\S6]{mumford1965picard}; \citet[Introduction, equations~(1-3)--(1-7)]{fultonolsson2010picard}; the analytic generator is \citet[Theorem~6.6]{hain2008moduli}.""",status='the Hodge-line interpretation and its order-twelve relation are established.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',refs=er+['fultonolsson2010picard'])
i(154,'P000252','construction','Shafarevich: a site of transverse patches and holonomy',r"""For an ordinary differential equation, objects are transverse patches and maps follow trajectories to another patch. A fibre product is drawn.""",r"""For a nonsingular smooth foliation, choose a complete transversal \(T\). Germs of holonomy maps between open subsets of \(T\) form an \(\acute{e}\)tale groupoid \(G\). Equivariant sheaves on \(T\), or the corresponding local site with ordinary open covers and holonomy identifications, retain the transverse topology and isotropy of the foliation. Fibre products of local transverse maps exist locally after restricting patches. Singular points require an additional definition and are not covered by this nonsingular construction.""",r"""The holonomy-groupoid interpretation makes the drawing precise but is a modern reconstruction. The note does not say whether path data, holonomy germs or only pointwise trajectory incidence define its morphisms; those choices can produce different groupoids.""",cls='B',fate=r"""A precise later development is \citet[\S\S1.1,2.1--2.2 and Theorem~3.1]{moerdijk1991foliations}: the classifying topos of an \(\acute{e}\)tale groupoid agrees in homotopy and cohomology with its classifying space. This matches the transverse-site method, not the unqualified singular-flow or Euler formula in the next remarks. The paper appeared in 1991; no earlier circulation date was verified.""",status='the nonsingular holonomy-site construction is established; the exact morphism convention of this notebook example remains only partially identified.',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',refs=['moerdijk1991foliations'],confidence='medium',speaker='I. R. Shafarevich')
i(154,'P000252','claim','For a genuine leaf fibration the transverse cohomology is that of the base',r"""The abbreviated sentence says that, for a fibration, the cohomology of the base and the system are \ru{трив.}, and mentions a final object.""",r"""If a foliation is the foliation by connected fibres of a surjective smooth submersion \(p:M\to B\) with manifold leaf space \(B\), its holonomy groupoid on a complete transversal is equivalent to the \(\acute{e}\)tale groupoid presenting \(B\). Its classifying topos is therefore equivalent to ordinary sheaves on \(B\), and the corresponding cohomology groups agree. They need not vanish: a nonempty connected base has \(H^0(B,\mathbb Z)=\mathbb Z\).""",r"""The abbreviation may mean that the identification is trivial, rather than that both cohomologies vanish. The Russian and scan do not decide the omitted predicate. The existence of a terminal object by itself never forces positive-degree cohomology to vanish.""",fate=r"""The groupoid invariance is \citet[\S2.2]{moerdijk1991foliations}; for a groupoid presenting a space the classifying topos is its usual sheaf topos, as explained in \S2.4 of that paper. The fibration case follows directly.""",status='the base comparison is established; a reading asserting vanishing is false and the notebook wording remains ambiguous.',literal='not determinable',match='partial',refs=['moerdijk1991foliations'],confidence='medium',speaker='I. R. Shafarevich')
i(154,'P000252','claim','Euler characteristic as a difference of cycle and singular-point counts',r"""For an equation on the plane and the constant sheaf of its Grothendieck topology, the Euler characteristic is said to be the difference between the number of limit cycles and the number of singular points.""",r"""No precise theorem is determined without specifying the vector-field class, treatment of singular leaves, cohomology theory and support condition, finiteness of the relevant groups, and which signed difference is intended. For example, for the constant nonzero horizontal vector field on \(\mathbb R^2\), the ordinary transverse leaf space is \(\mathbb R\), with ordinary constant-sheaf Euler characteristic one, while both stated counts are zero. Thus the unrestricted ordinary-cohomology interpretation is false.""",r"""The counterexample does not identify the author's intended compact-support, reduced or relative invariant. It demonstrates why the omitted convention is substantive. The phrase does not supply a definite open problem, and no problem-index entry is invented.""",cls='F',fate=r"""Searches combining the named speaker, transverse sites, Euler characteristic, singular points and limit cycles did not identify a publication with these exact hypotheses and conclusion. \citet[Theorem~3.1]{moerdijk1991foliations} provides the general cohomological framework but no such cycle-count formula.""",status='the intended invariant and theorem remain unidentified; the broad ordinary-cohomology reading is refuted by the stated elementary example.',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',refs=['moerdijk1991foliations'],confidence='low',speaker='I. R. Shafarevich')
